19
00:21:13,880 --> 00:21:23,520
So there's actually a general long-standing principle of English law that says that a pure penalty clause is not enforceable.

20
00:21:23,520 --> 00:21:25,840
All right, but then aren't there penalty clauses?

21
00:21:25,840 --> 00:21:29,440
Well, don't call it a penalty clause, first of all, if you want it to be enforced.

22
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:36,480
And then you need to go and pretend, some way of pretending that it's not really a penalty, that it's just a

23
00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:44,200
compensation for your enormous losses, which could be, however, totally psychological, for example, if you set it up right.

24
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:46,400
All right.

25
00:21:46,400 --> 00:21:50,960
So, you know, if you could go and change the payoffs of the game by writing a contract,

26
00:21:51,200 --> 00:21:54,480
this would greatly enhance the ability to have cooperation.

27
00:21:55,120 --> 00:21:58,720
Right, so in the 19th century, before there were antitrust laws,

28
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:05,960
there were still some court rulings saying that a collusive contract is contrary to public policy, and though, while it is not illegal,

29
00:22:05,960 --> 00:22:07,960
it's also not enforceable.

30
00:22:08,840 --> 00:22:12,480
So an enforceable one would be one where if you go and cheat on your quota,

31
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:15,760
you can be sued by the other people that you signed the contract with.

32
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:21,320
And what these pre-antitrust cases said is, no, you can't actually sue someone for that. You can write the contract,

33
00:22:21,480 --> 00:22:26,680
you can try to do whatever you want to privately enforce it, but the courts are not going to help you do it.

34
00:22:26,680 --> 00:22:33,840
Now, Robin Hanson actually has a dissertation chapter on the puzzle of why is it the legal systems,

35
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:38,160
especially the Anglo-American one, don't allow for pure punishment?

36
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:41,520
Because, at least on the surface, punishment is awesome.

37
00:22:42,560 --> 00:22:48,960
Right, it's a way of overcoming all the problems of cooperation that simple repeat don't solve.

38
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:55,680
Now, of course, a classic objection to this is say, well, someone might then sign a contract where they agree to a punishment,

39
00:22:55,680 --> 00:23:00,320
and then the other side will go and lie and punish them in a way that was not really appropriate.

40
00:23:00,800 --> 00:23:08,800
For example, you could have an employment contract that says that if you ever slack on the job, that you owe the boss a million dollars.

41
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:14,400
Now, once you sign that contract, you should be worried that your boss will do something to you.

42
00:23:14,400 --> 00:23:19,120
Now, once you sign that contract, you should be worried that your boss will do what?

43
00:23:19,120 --> 00:23:21,120
Try to catch you.

44
00:23:21,120 --> 00:23:24,800
Yes, try to catch you, or that by itself isn't that bad, just don't do it.

45
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:27,760
But the real worry is, you know, frame you.

46
00:23:27,760 --> 00:23:31,200
Lie, claim that you did it just to go and steal a million dollars from you.

47
00:23:31,200 --> 00:23:40,000
But, of course, the obvious response to this fear is if you don't like that contract, don't sign it.

48
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:46,080
Right? So that is the obvious market check upon abusive contracts of this kind,

49
00:23:46,080 --> 00:23:50,080
is just to say, look, the contracts are legal, they're enforceable, but be warned.

50
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:58,160
If you do make the benefit, or the punishment, that great for one side, then they may actually be tempted to

51
00:23:58,160 --> 00:24:02,880
try to frame you or lie, or otherwise lie, in order to go and capture the benefits.

52
00:24:02,880 --> 00:24:06,400
But given that a person is signing this, there's no reason not to enforce it.

53
00:24:06,400 --> 00:24:12,480
It's a reason to be cautious before you sign, but it's not a reason to say that you can't actually have contracts like this.

54
00:24:12,480 --> 00:24:16,480
And yet the law actually does generally say these contracts are not enforceable.

55
00:24:16,480 --> 00:24:24,960
Which, if you think about different alternative arrangements for certifying worker quality, might be a big deal.

56
00:24:24,960 --> 00:24:31,200
So right now, if say, you have no academic degrees at all, and you want to get a job, you might make the offer.

57
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:35,840
Give me a chance, I'll work for free, and if you don't like me, then you can fire me.

58
00:24:35,840 --> 00:24:42,880
So even that, there's a little bit of an issue, because there's some rules about unpaid internships, but you can probably get away with that.

59
00:24:42,880 --> 00:24:48,800
But if you signed a contract saying, if you aren't happy with me, I owe you $10,000,

60
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:54,160
which is still less than a year's tuition at most places, so it could be a realistic...

61
00:24:54,160 --> 00:24:58,880
If this is the way you get your foot in the door at a job, this could still be a way better deal than going to college for this.

62
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:01,520
But this contract would generally not be enforced.

63
00:25:01,520 --> 00:25:11,920
So, what would be a quick little legal definition of what the theory of pure punishment is?

64
00:25:11,920 --> 00:25:20,000
Because I'm trying to see if I can distinguish that between saying, if you hurt someone, or you fail to fulfill a contract, then you're owed money damages.

65
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:27,360
Right, so the normal way the contract law works is that you are only liable for an amount of damages equal to whatever financial loss you caused.

66
00:25:27,360 --> 00:25:30,480
A punishment would be one where you owe more than that.

67
00:25:30,480 --> 00:25:35,600
So you owe some multiple of the damage that you caused.

68
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:39,440
Right, and why would you ever need a multiple?

69
00:25:39,440 --> 00:25:46,240
Well, just most obviously, you might need a multiple in order to compensate you for your court costs or your legal expenses.

70
00:25:46,240 --> 00:25:50,080
So the way that current contract law works is...

71
00:25:50,080 --> 00:25:58,000
There is some way to recover your legal costs, but normally the way that works is if someone breaches a contract that costs you a million dollars,

72
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:02,240
you hire a lawyer that costs you a lot of money, and then if you win, you get a million dollars back.

73
00:26:02,240 --> 00:26:10,160
As I was mentioning, antitrust law gives you treble damages, so that is actually a statutory punishment that's worked in.

74
00:26:10,160 --> 00:26:14,080
But that's actually one that you cannot contractually waive either.

75
00:26:14,080 --> 00:26:21,040
So you can't say, I'm your franchisee, a condition of getting a franchise from Carl's Jr. is that I can't sue you under the antitrust laws.

76
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:23,040
No, that will not be enforced.

77
00:26:23,040 --> 00:26:26,160
That rule will not be binding.

78
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:35,600
So we're going to get Robin has a whole chapter which goes over what I'd say is a fascinating issue of why is it that voluntary punishments are so heavily...

79
00:26:35,600 --> 00:26:39,040
Or are so discouraged by the legal system.

80
00:26:39,040 --> 00:26:48,160
There are, as I said, there are a number of loopholes that you can try to get it in, but the official position still is no one should be able to consent to be punished.

81
00:26:48,160 --> 00:26:53,120
Even though there's lots of reasons why it would make a lot of sense to consent to be punished.

82
00:26:53,120 --> 00:27:01,280
And individually, you probably would very much want to consent to be punished so that you could persuade people to deal with you.

83
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:15,440
So anytime someone is nervous about you and you haven't managed to win them over, saying, look, I will go and offer you this extra favorable term in the contract that I am going to owe you a lot of money if you're ever dissatisfied.

84
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:19,120
All right, now as to how stores get away with double your money back.

85
00:27:19,120 --> 00:27:26,160
So that might not be legally enforceable either, although that's one where since it's the consumer against a big, it's an evil corporation or something.

86
00:27:26,160 --> 00:27:32,080
That's one where probably they would somehow juggle the books in order to make you pay.

87
00:27:32,080 --> 00:27:36,880
All right, but so when we get to the chapter on asymmetric over the south, we can asymmetric information.

88
00:27:36,880 --> 00:27:43,360
That's the best story anyone has come up with for what the point of banning punishment clauses is.

89
00:27:43,360 --> 00:27:45,120
But we'll talk about the later on.

90
00:27:45,120 --> 00:27:50,240
Anyway, it's an interesting question.

91
00:27:50,240 --> 00:28:01,120
All right, now when you do experiments, what we see is that cooperation is greater than either repeated play or even cozy bargaining would allow.

92
00:28:01,120 --> 00:28:10,640
And the obvious explanation, shocking as it may seem, is that at least some of the time human beings are less selfish than economists assume.

93
00:28:10,640 --> 00:28:13,040
All right, oh my God.

94
00:28:13,040 --> 00:28:14,720
No way.

95
00:28:14,720 --> 00:28:17,680
All right, but anyway, that's the obvious explanation.

96
00:28:17,680 --> 00:28:24,160
Ron Heiner, who is in the office next to me, has a 300-page book with another explanation, which I do not rightly understand.

97
00:28:24,160 --> 00:28:34,800
But again, the simplest explanation is that people are just not as selfish as a simple model would predict.

98
00:28:34,800 --> 00:28:38,480
Okay, questions?

99
00:28:38,480 --> 00:28:47,520
All right, now last point, if you think that this extra cooperation is motivated by something like altruism or fairness or some kind of conscience,

100
00:28:47,520 --> 00:28:57,520
then there is a reason to think that there's actually in the real world a difference between socially harmful collusion and socially beneficial cooperation,

101
00:28:57,520 --> 00:28:59,920
even though in game theoretic terms they're the same thing.

102
00:28:59,920 --> 00:29:10,160
So in game theoretic terms, going and getting cooperation in this situation is exactly the same as getting cooperation in the Cournot model.

103
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:17,840
Right, in both cases, you would go and use punishments in order to threaten people and repeat a game in order to cooperate.

104
00:29:17,840 --> 00:29:23,760
In this case, say, look, if you don't donate enough, like we're all going to stop donating and the whole system is going to break down, so you better watch it.

105
00:29:23,760 --> 00:29:25,360
You better donate enough.

106
00:29:25,360 --> 00:29:30,480
And in the Cournot model, it would be you better restrict your production like other people are doing,

107
00:29:30,480 --> 00:29:36,560
because if you don't, then we're all going to produce the regular Cournot amount, and then we're all going to be worse off, including you, so you better not try that.

108
00:29:36,560 --> 00:29:45,600
Okay, now since, as I said, we generally see more cooperation than gets predicted by either repeat or co's,

109
00:29:45,600 --> 00:29:51,920
and since the obvious explanation is that people are just not as selfish as the model predicts,

110
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:58,160
then there's actually a reason to be optimistic that there actually, in the real world, is a difference between this and Cournot.

111
00:29:58,160 --> 00:30:04,960
Namely, in a situation like this, if someone goes and cheats, what does their conscience tell them?

112
00:30:04,960 --> 00:30:11,440
You're bad. You're being bad. Don't do that. Don't cheat. Good people don't do that.

113
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:18,720
Go and be a good person who goes and not only cooperates as much as in your interest, but actually do more than that.

114
00:30:18,720 --> 00:30:24,160
On the other hand, if you are all trying to collude to restrict the world's supply of aspirin,

115
00:30:24,160 --> 00:30:34,560
even though in purely formal terms it's the same problem, it is unlikely that people doing it feel really guilty when they go and produce extra aspirin.

116
00:30:34,560 --> 00:30:44,880
So there actually was an aspirin price fixing case, and as far as I can tell from the back story, no one bothered appealing to anyone's conscience.

117
00:30:44,880 --> 00:30:49,840
There wasn't anyone saying, look, we all know that it's a better world if we restrict aspirin, right?

118
00:30:49,840 --> 00:30:54,640
Well, can't you be a good person and just stop producing so much aspirin?

119
00:30:54,640 --> 00:31:00,720
So that was not the appeal. Instead, the appeal was, well, we all want to make more money for ourselves, right?

120
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:06,880
And then there's a whole system of kickbacks that are paid and bonuses to people to keep their mouths shut,

121
00:31:06,880 --> 00:31:11,920
all suggesting a certain degree of guilty feelings among the participants.

122
00:31:11,920 --> 00:31:15,360
And yes, the reason we know about it, of course, is it not actually lasts.

123
00:31:15,360 --> 00:31:22,320
There are actually some Swiss executives from Bayer who got deported to serve jail time in Texas in the end.

124
00:31:22,320 --> 00:31:24,720
So, bad outcome for them.

125
00:31:24,720 --> 00:31:30,080
Does this suggest that union collusion is more likely to be sustainable?

126
00:31:30,080 --> 00:31:31,280
Yes, yes, great question.

127
00:31:31,280 --> 00:31:32,480
Because they have a moral...

128
00:31:32,480 --> 00:31:36,240
Yes, yes, so sometimes people do manage to moralize collusion.

129
00:31:36,240 --> 00:31:43,280
So unions are the most obvious and famous case of this, where yes, there is a whole ideology of someone

130
00:31:43,280 --> 00:31:51,280
who violates the union is not just hurting the group, but is actually a rat scab.

131
00:31:51,280 --> 00:31:53,520
Right? You have two things that are both bad.

132
00:31:53,520 --> 00:31:55,520
Right?

133
00:31:55,520 --> 00:31:56,720
I don't know.

134
00:31:56,720 --> 00:32:02,000
White shirt likes to be like, I was told that picking scabs is bad, but I don't know, somehow, scab is an insult.

135
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:09,840
So scab is an insult for someone who breaks a union, who doesn't participate in a union, or works when the union says don't.

136
00:32:09,840 --> 00:32:14,800
I did see a May Day parade in New York City where there was about a 12 foot inflatable rat.

137
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:22,000
Not a cute rat, but like a really, I guess, like a horrible, ugly rat, and it said don't be a rat scab.

138
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:28,240
So I looked in New York, and actually whenever there are union disputes, unions will pay to put that in front of a building

139
00:32:28,240 --> 00:32:30,800
where it's being painted, or something is being installed.

140
00:32:30,800 --> 00:32:34,000
So there's a small industry for these giant inflatable rats.

141
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:36,000
Or is there just one rat?

142
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:40,800
So one rat who gets moved around, like a kid's party inflatable?

143
00:32:40,800 --> 00:32:43,600
I've seen multiple for multiple unions.

144
00:32:43,600 --> 00:32:45,600
Yeah.

145
00:32:45,600 --> 00:32:52,080
So yeah, so if you can manage to moralize collusion, then it's going to work like other kinds of,

146
00:32:52,080 --> 00:32:54,960
then it will work much more with charity.

147
00:32:54,960 --> 00:33:02,400
But for the large area of collusion where people just think of it as a way to make an extra buck, and they don't feel morally superior to other people,

148
00:33:02,400 --> 00:33:11,120
I suggest that that is not going to work nearly as well as the kind of cooperation where people feel like there is a general good that is being achieved.

149
00:33:11,120 --> 00:33:20,400
And if you're thinking about how optimistic is this, a lot of it depends on what is the correlation between people's perception of whether they're being good and actual social good.

150
00:33:20,400 --> 00:33:27,600
So presumably when people are collecting money for orphans, almost everybody thinks that's good, and I suggest that probably actually is good.

151
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:38,160
And on the other hand, when people are trying to restrict the production of aspirin, sounds pretty bad.

152
00:33:38,160 --> 00:33:46,400
For food, actually, that is another case where before there were official government farm cartels,

153
00:33:46,400 --> 00:33:53,760
there was a lot of moralizing of the desirability of restricting food production in order to raise prices, but that was one that never worked.

154
00:33:53,760 --> 00:33:57,600
Probably because the moralizing just didn't really actually take that much.

155
00:33:57,600 --> 00:34:09,440
Why? Well, I'm not a social psychologist, but it seems very likely to me that your feeling of guilt is going to be very different if you're betraying people that you see every day,

156
00:34:09,440 --> 00:34:16,400
versus if you are on a farm where you were running your own farm and then you get a letter in the mail saying, please restrict production,

157
00:34:16,400 --> 00:34:20,960
come to the town meeting, and then you see the monks, and then you go back and you're on your farm again and no one else is around.

158
00:34:20,960 --> 00:34:27,200
And you're like, well, I don't know why it would be so bad for me to go and throw some more food on these perfectly empty acres here.

159
00:34:27,200 --> 00:34:35,120
And even during the Great Depression, as you may recall, there was a lot of moralizing of the evil of destroying food at a time people were hungry.

160
00:34:35,120 --> 00:34:44,720
I just remember about the idea, like, today we do the relatively efficient kind of restricting food production,

161
00:34:44,720 --> 00:34:47,760
where the government tries to stop the food from being created in the first place,

162
00:34:47,760 --> 00:34:52,080
but during the Great Depression, the chaos was so great that it got grown and then it got destroyed afterwards,

163
00:34:52,080 --> 00:34:58,320
which tends to really upset people, especially during a time of poverty and hunger and everything else.

164
00:34:58,320 --> 00:35:06,000
So anyway, I do have a paper with Ed Stringham where we build on this point, where we are arguing against Tyler Cowen,

165
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:14,000
who says that either you should be pessimistic about charity and collusion, thinking that private charity will not be much,

166
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:19,600
but that collusion will be much, or you should be optimistic about both, in the sense that you should think that private charity works well,

167
00:35:19,600 --> 00:35:25,840
but also collusion is going to be strong. And we suggest that actually there is a reason to think there's an asymmetry,

168
00:35:25,840 --> 00:35:33,680
namely that the moralizing of cooperation makes a difference. So that is our story, if you're curious.

169
00:35:33,680 --> 00:35:41,200
All right, questions? All right. So let's now do some more on coordination.

170
00:35:41,200 --> 00:35:45,680
So remember we had this very simple coordination game that we've been going over quite a bit,

171
00:35:45,680 --> 00:35:50,160
where you've got people, say, driving on the left and right-hand side of the road,

172
00:35:50,160 --> 00:35:59,760
and they can either coordinate on driving on the left side, which could be socially inferior because people are biologically right-handed,

173
00:35:59,760 --> 00:36:05,520
say, most people, 90%, or they can both coordinate to be on the right side,

174
00:36:05,520 --> 00:36:10,880
or we could have the really bad outcome where we don't coordinate and then we have bad payoffs for everybody.

175
00:36:10,880 --> 00:36:21,040
And as you recall, we have two pure strategy Nash equilibria, PSME, and then there's also a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium.

176
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:27,520
There's a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium where if you go and solve it, you'll see that everybody could randomize

177
00:36:27,520 --> 00:36:32,000
just at exactly the right levels so that you're indifferent between playing left and right.

178
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:37,680
And then you get some very bad social outcomes because if both sides are randomizing,

179
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:42,160
then there's a pretty good chance that those diagonals will occur.

180
00:36:42,160 --> 00:36:44,160
And those diagonals are really bad.

181
00:36:44,160 --> 00:36:47,120
So imagine people randomly deciding which side of the road to drive on.

182
00:36:47,120 --> 00:36:49,760
Not a very good outcome.

183
00:36:49,760 --> 00:36:53,520
Now the interesting thing about that bad mixed strategy Nash equilibrium,

184
00:36:53,520 --> 00:36:57,280
I'm not going to prove it mathematically, but I'm going to try to explain it intuitively,

185
00:36:57,280 --> 00:37:02,480
is that in this game, in the coordination game, not as a general rule, but in the coordination game,

186
00:37:02,480 --> 00:37:04,960
the mixed strategy Nash equilibrium is unstable.

187
00:37:04,960 --> 00:37:06,080
What does this mean?

188
00:37:06,080 --> 00:37:10,080
It means that if you go a little bit above or a little bit below the probabilities,

189
00:37:10,080 --> 00:37:13,040
instead of naturally going back to the mixed strategy probabilities,

190
00:37:13,040 --> 00:37:15,920
you naturally go as far away from them as possible.

191
00:37:15,920 --> 00:37:24,080
So think about this being, say, Israel and you're trying to decide whether the national language is going to be English or Hebrew.

192
00:37:24,080 --> 00:37:24,800
All right.

193
00:37:24,800 --> 00:37:28,240
Now, when would you be indifferent?

194
00:37:28,240 --> 00:37:32,720
You're indifferent if you have exactly, like, as soon as your languages are equally easy to learn,

195
00:37:32,720 --> 00:37:37,840
and you show up not knowing either one, then you'll be indifferent between the two languages

196
00:37:37,840 --> 00:37:42,400
if exactly 50% speaks English and exactly 50% speaks Hebrew.

197
00:37:42,400 --> 00:37:47,440
But suppose that 51% of the population speaks Hebrew.

198
00:37:47,440 --> 00:37:49,360
Then which language is the better one to learn?

199
00:37:49,360 --> 00:37:50,000
Hebrew.

200
00:37:50,000 --> 00:37:50,720
Hebrew.

201
00:37:50,720 --> 00:37:57,040
And since all the new people want to switch to that one, what does that do to the fraction speaking Hebrew?

202
00:37:57,040 --> 00:37:57,920
Increase it.

203
00:37:57,920 --> 00:37:59,760
Which makes it even more clear that you want to speak Hebrew,

204
00:37:59,760 --> 00:38:02,000
which makes it even more clear that you want to speak Hebrew,

205
00:38:02,000 --> 00:38:06,080
which then naturally moves you to the equilibrium where everyone is speaking Hebrew.

206
00:38:06,080 --> 00:38:10,720
Similarly, if you were at this one where people are just evenly—

207
00:38:10,720 --> 00:38:12,880
Actually, we can make this really easy.

208
00:38:12,880 --> 00:38:14,720
You can even do it like this.

209
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:16,720
You can do—

210
00:38:16,720 --> 00:38:17,920
Imagine we did 5-5.

211
00:38:17,920 --> 00:38:21,200
So here it's clear that left and right are both equally good.

212
00:38:21,200 --> 00:38:24,880
So you can start off in a world where half the people are driving the left-hand side of the road

213
00:38:24,880 --> 00:38:26,800
and half are driving the right-hand side of the road.

214
00:38:26,800 --> 00:38:30,480
And if you're just one person, there isn't any good side anymore.

215
00:38:30,480 --> 00:38:32,640
Each side is deadly.

216
00:38:32,640 --> 00:38:37,360
You just hope that you happen to be driving on the same side as the other people you encounter.

217
00:38:37,360 --> 00:38:41,040
But the good news is as soon as there is a slight tipping,

218
00:38:41,040 --> 00:38:45,120
as soon as 51% of people are driving on the right-hand side of the road,

219
00:38:45,120 --> 00:38:48,160
then clearly the smart bet is to drive on the right-hand side of the road,

220
00:38:48,160 --> 00:38:50,880
which makes it even clearer for everybody else that it's smart to go on the right,

221
00:38:50,880 --> 00:38:54,000
which makes it even more clear, and then you go off to right.

222
00:38:54,000 --> 00:38:58,160
Or of course, if the probability were 49%,

223
00:38:58,160 --> 00:39:02,480
people would go to left instead.

224
00:39:02,480 --> 00:39:05,360
And this is in general how coordination games work.

225
00:39:05,360 --> 00:39:10,080
So while there is a mixed strategy and Nash equilibrium,

226
00:39:10,080 --> 00:39:15,760
mathematically these are unstable, meaning that while you could stay there if you were exactly there,

227
00:39:15,760 --> 00:39:20,080
your odds of remaining there are very slim because the slightest perturbation

228
00:39:20,080 --> 00:39:24,000
will move the whole system to one extreme outcome or the other.

229
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:26,240
Just like standing a coin on its side.

230
00:39:26,240 --> 00:39:29,920
That is an equilibrium, but it's not stable.

231
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:32,800
So a small breeze can go and tip the coin over.

232
00:39:32,800 --> 00:39:34,720
Anyone ever tried doing this?

233
00:39:34,720 --> 00:39:39,680
There's a Twilight Zone episode where somehow coin landing on a side leads to telepathy.

234
00:39:39,680 --> 00:39:42,880
But I don't think that's true.

235
00:39:42,880 --> 00:39:45,760
Although whenever I see a coin on its side, it's always been placed there.

236
00:39:45,760 --> 00:39:48,880
I've never actually seen it land there, which is really hard.

237
00:39:48,880 --> 00:39:51,920
But obviously it's an equilibrium because you can do it.

238
00:39:51,920 --> 00:39:54,240
You can put a coin on its side and it'll stay there,

239
00:39:54,240 --> 00:39:58,320
which means that if you flip the coin ever just so, just perfectly,

240
00:39:58,320 --> 00:40:26,160
that it would stand there on its side.


1
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:02,800
but you probably go for a long time without it happening.

2
00:40:04,160 --> 00:40:04,380
All right.

3
00:40:05,140 --> 00:40:08,560
So, questions about just the basics of coordination games?

4
00:40:09,560 --> 00:40:09,780
All right.

5
00:40:10,200 --> 00:40:12,040
Now, there's lots of real world applications

6
00:40:12,040 --> 00:40:13,600
of coordination games we're thinking about.

7
00:40:14,060 --> 00:40:15,880
So, like I already suggested, you've got language.

8
00:40:15,880 --> 00:40:16,680
All right.

9
00:40:16,680 --> 00:40:19,960
So, right now, linguists will say that we are in the midst

10
00:40:19,960 --> 00:40:21,820
of a great linguistic die off and most

11
00:40:21,820 --> 00:40:23,940
of the world's languages are disappearing right now.

12
00:40:24,780 --> 00:40:24,960
All right.

13
00:40:25,060 --> 00:40:27,340
And from a coordination perspective,

14
00:40:27,780 --> 00:40:29,160
there's a lot to recommend this.

15
00:40:29,160 --> 00:40:30,720
Right? It's like, well, what's so great

16
00:40:30,720 --> 00:40:32,560
about preserving language when only 50 people speak?

17
00:40:33,240 --> 00:40:34,660
Like, what can you do with the language?

18
00:40:35,100 --> 00:40:38,080
And like, however beautiful the language is, there's not likely

19
00:40:38,080 --> 00:40:39,380
to be much worth reading

20
00:40:39,380 --> 00:40:41,180
in a language only spoken by 50 people.

21
00:40:42,180 --> 00:40:44,100
Right? So, what movies are going to be seen by,

22
00:40:44,100 --> 00:40:46,660
or going to be made by, in a language

23
00:40:46,660 --> 00:40:48,020
where only 50 people speak it?

24
00:40:48,020 --> 00:40:51,680
So, it doesn't seem like there's that much of a point to it.

25
00:40:51,680 --> 00:40:53,760
But anyway, of course, the value, the whole idea,

26
00:40:53,760 --> 00:40:55,620
the value of a language depends upon the number

27
00:40:55,620 --> 00:40:56,620
of people speaking it.

28
00:40:56,620 --> 00:40:59,300
If you invent a new language, it could be fantastic in every way,

29
00:40:59,300 --> 00:41:02,300
except that no one else speaks it and then that's enough

30
00:41:02,300 --> 00:41:03,160
to make it worthless.

31
00:41:03,640 --> 00:41:05,080
Right? Almost worthless anyway.

32
00:41:05,080 --> 00:41:06,580
All right.

33
00:41:06,580 --> 00:41:08,580
So, language is a pretty obvious application

34
00:41:08,580 --> 00:41:09,800
of the coordination game.

35
00:41:09,800 --> 00:41:12,260
Another one is, you know, so culture more broadly.

36
00:41:13,100 --> 00:41:15,780
So, there are all sorts of cultural norms that exist,

37
00:41:16,280 --> 00:41:18,380
where the important thing is just that there be a norm.

38
00:41:18,920 --> 00:41:20,380
And having a norm is more important

39
00:41:20,380 --> 00:41:21,680
than exactly what the norm is.

40
00:41:22,180 --> 00:41:23,680
So, for example, if you were familiar

41
00:41:23,680 --> 00:41:25,980
with Eastern European culture, as I am,

42
00:41:25,980 --> 00:41:30,380
since my wife is from Romania, you know that they,

43
00:41:30,380 --> 00:41:32,320
that at a meal they will vary,

44
00:41:32,320 --> 00:41:33,800
they will struggle to give you food.

45
00:41:34,440 --> 00:41:36,100
Right? They'll keep trying to put food on your plate

46
00:41:36,100 --> 00:41:38,660
and feed you and it's considered rude not to offer.

47
00:41:39,740 --> 00:41:43,820
Right? Now, you know, among native-born Americans,

48
00:41:43,820 --> 00:41:46,820
it's, you know, the other rule is usually in place, where no,

49
00:41:47,080 --> 00:41:48,800
people go and take the food that they want

50
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:49,800
and it's rude to push.

51
00:41:50,580 --> 00:41:51,580
Right? Now, if you have two people

52
00:41:51,580 --> 00:41:53,920
who both follow the Romanian custom, that works okay.

53
00:41:53,920 --> 00:41:56,020
Because then, you know, so like people don't ask,

54
00:41:56,020 --> 00:41:58,060
but you offer and so you get food that way.

55
00:41:58,460 --> 00:41:59,480
Or if you have two people both

56
00:41:59,480 --> 00:42:01,040
of the American custom, that works.

57
00:42:01,540 --> 00:42:03,740
Because you're going to, if people don't offer,

58
00:42:03,740 --> 00:42:06,280
but people are, people assert themselves and ask, that works.

59
00:42:06,740 --> 00:42:08,740
But if you combine an American with a Romanian,

60
00:42:08,740 --> 00:42:09,840
then you do have a conflict.

61
00:42:09,840 --> 00:42:13,340
Because if someone who says, look, I take what I want

62
00:42:13,340 --> 00:42:15,180
and like don't bother me and someone else says, no,

63
00:42:15,180 --> 00:42:18,600
you must take this, no thanks, yes, all right,

64
00:42:18,600 --> 00:42:19,680
that causes conflict.

65
00:42:20,040 --> 00:42:21,980
And of course, if you, if you reverse it,

66
00:42:21,980 --> 00:42:24,140
if you have someone who was embarrassed to ask for food

67
00:42:24,140 --> 00:42:25,620
and then someone who doesn't offer them food,

68
00:42:25,900 --> 00:42:26,940
then they wind up starving.

69
00:42:26,940 --> 00:42:29,160
So that also gives you a bad outcome.

70
00:42:29,160 --> 00:42:33,200
Let's see, I also remember philosopher John Searle talking

71
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:36,760
about different cultural views about the right distance

72
00:42:36,760 --> 00:42:38,760
between two human beings for a conversation.

73
00:42:39,900 --> 00:42:43,200
So he said, so the Italians, according to him,

74
00:42:43,200 --> 00:42:45,700
at least Italian philosophers, believe that a distance

75
00:42:45,700 --> 00:42:47,460
of about three inches is optimal.

76
00:42:47,460 --> 00:42:52,080
Whereas Britons believe a distance

77
00:42:52,080 --> 00:42:54,700
of about say 10 feet is about right.

78
00:42:55,300 --> 00:42:56,860
And he said that you could go

79
00:42:56,860 --> 00:42:58,300
to international philosophy conferences

80
00:42:58,300 --> 00:43:01,380
and see the Italian philosophers almost chasing the Britons.

81
00:43:01,380 --> 00:43:06,180
And the Italian tries to get close enough for a conversation

82
00:43:06,180 --> 00:43:09,340
and the Briton tries to get far enough away to talk to you.

83
00:43:10,660 --> 00:43:13,760
So, and we can back, so you can either have the close distance,

84
00:43:13,760 --> 00:43:15,800
is what people expect, or the far distance.

85
00:43:15,800 --> 00:43:17,060
Either one can work.

86
00:43:17,300 --> 00:43:20,920
And you can go and debate what the actually socially optimal

87
00:43:20,920 --> 00:43:24,360
culture is, but still having just a disagreement

88
00:43:24,360 --> 00:43:28,060
about what the cultural rule is poses extra problems above

89
00:43:28,060 --> 00:43:30,540
and beyond whatever flaws you see

90
00:43:30,540 --> 00:43:32,160
with a particular cultural rule.

91
00:43:33,100 --> 00:43:36,040
Right? And then obviously for culture that can include things

92
00:43:36,040 --> 00:43:37,700
like what clothes are acceptable to wear.

93
00:43:37,700 --> 00:43:40,080
So let's see, no two people in this room are wearing the same

94
00:43:40,080 --> 00:43:42,300
clothes, but no one is wearing anything

95
00:43:42,740 --> 00:43:45,160
that would shock anyone, I don't think.

96
00:43:45,160 --> 00:43:49,440
Some people are shocked by a professor with flip flops.

97
00:43:49,440 --> 00:43:53,240
But over time this has become less shocking.

98
00:43:53,240 --> 00:43:54,240
All right?

99
00:43:54,240 --> 00:43:56,840
So, you know, but if one person were wearing say a Star Trek

100
00:43:56,840 --> 00:44:00,560
uniform, there was, I think it was actually one

101
00:44:00,560 --> 00:44:02,940
of the Clinton trials involved a juror

102
00:44:02,940 --> 00:44:05,080
who always wore her Star Trek uniform every day.

103
00:44:05,080 --> 00:44:08,200
So this was many, like 20 years ago.

104
00:44:08,200 --> 00:44:10,080
And then interviewed said, well, you know,

105
00:44:10,280 --> 00:44:11,480
as a Starfleet officer.

106
00:44:11,480 --> 00:44:18,120
So it doesn't break character or, you know,

107
00:44:18,120 --> 00:44:19,700
maybe she is a Starfleet officer.

108
00:44:19,700 --> 00:44:20,680
It's unclear.

109
00:44:21,460 --> 00:44:26,400
So, you know, so there are, you know, coordination norms

110
00:44:26,400 --> 00:44:27,740
about what is acceptable attire.

111
00:44:28,080 --> 00:44:30,420
You may have noticed a common question that people ask

112
00:44:30,420 --> 00:44:33,440
when they're going to a important social event is,

113
00:44:33,440 --> 00:44:35,060
well, what are we supposed to wear?

114
00:44:35,360 --> 00:44:36,360
What are you wearing?

115
00:44:36,640 --> 00:44:38,060
So people are trying, if not,

116
00:44:38,060 --> 00:44:40,640
to exactly match each other's clothes to go and coordinate

117
00:44:40,640 --> 00:44:43,120
on what would be typical at that time.

118
00:44:43,120 --> 00:44:45,600
And it's not always formal attire.

119
00:44:45,600 --> 00:44:47,120
So sometimes people would be embarrassed

120
00:44:47,120 --> 00:44:49,080
to be dressed formally when everyone else is informal.

121
00:44:49,080 --> 00:44:50,760
I just said that.

122
00:44:50,760 --> 00:44:52,360
I was like, overdressed.

123
00:44:52,640 --> 00:44:53,760
It's so embarrassing.

124
00:44:54,480 --> 00:44:54,760
All right.

125
00:44:55,120 --> 00:44:57,760
So there's that.

126
00:44:57,760 --> 00:45:00,220
Then another very common application

127
00:45:00,220 --> 00:45:02,440
of coordination games, especially in economics

128
00:45:02,440 --> 00:45:04,500
but also public policy is technology.

129
00:45:04,920 --> 00:45:08,080
So I already mentioned that you've got a lot of,

130
00:45:08,080 --> 00:45:10,720
there's only a whole academic literature on things

131
00:45:10,720 --> 00:45:15,360
like the QWERTY keyboard and VHS versus Betamax for videotapes

132
00:45:15,820 --> 00:45:19,920
and many other examples in technology where a key part

133
00:45:19,920 --> 00:45:22,700
of the technology is just being compatible with other items.

134
00:45:23,280 --> 00:45:26,640
So I guess it was 10 years ago I was teaching this class waiting

135
00:45:26,640 --> 00:45:29,480
for a high-definition DVD standard to emerge

136
00:45:29,920 --> 00:45:33,600
because for a couple years there was Blu-ray and HD DVD.

137
00:45:34,080 --> 00:45:35,480
And there's a format war.

138
00:45:35,480 --> 00:45:38,740
And I was, I like most people that were even interested

139
00:45:38,740 --> 00:45:40,860
in it was holding off for one to die.

140
00:45:42,140 --> 00:45:45,100
And then HD DVD died even though it had a better name.

141
00:45:45,620 --> 00:45:48,220
But Blu, but so I think the key thing was Blu,

142
00:45:48,220 --> 00:45:51,020
it was Walmart said we're dumping HD DVD

143
00:45:51,020 --> 00:45:51,780
and then that was it.

144
00:45:52,300 --> 00:45:54,900
So Walmart just, Walmart seemed to be the key actor

145
00:45:55,400 --> 00:45:56,180
that changed things.

146
00:45:56,700 --> 00:45:59,780
And there were people saying that HD DVD was superior

147
00:45:59,780 --> 00:46:00,600
for something or other.

148
00:46:00,600 --> 00:46:01,980
I can't remember.

149
00:46:01,980 --> 00:46:03,980
I think Blu-ray was better for games

150
00:46:03,980 --> 00:46:06,500
and HD DVD was supposed to be better for some other purpose.

151
00:46:07,260 --> 00:46:08,100
I guess like movies.

152
00:46:08,220 --> 00:46:11,460
But I mean I suppose I can imagine Blu-rays being better

153
00:46:11,460 --> 00:46:15,340
for movies but hard to see that it's that much of a gain.

154
00:46:15,340 --> 00:46:17,360
But the main thing was just to have some standard

155
00:46:17,900 --> 00:46:19,660
so that you would know that you were not going

156
00:46:19,660 --> 00:46:22,120
and wasting your money on getting the wrong kind of player.

157
00:46:23,340 --> 00:46:25,460
And remember initially the players liked 400 bucks.

158
00:46:25,940 --> 00:46:27,580
So it was not just a trivial question.

159
00:46:27,580 --> 00:46:29,260
It was not just the cost of one or two discs.

160
00:46:30,720 --> 00:46:31,020
All right.

161
00:46:31,080 --> 00:46:33,420
And then the last one that Paul Krugman keeps trying

162
00:46:33,420 --> 00:46:34,440
to popularize me.

163
00:46:34,440 --> 00:46:37,280
So he's one of the people who manages to be,

164
00:46:37,280 --> 00:46:39,460
to feel like very neglected despite a Nobel Prize.

165
00:46:39,460 --> 00:46:44,020
So it's one of those great skills that you have

166
00:46:44,020 --> 00:46:44,740
to practice.

167
00:46:44,740 --> 00:46:47,820
Like to feel like unjustly neglected.

168
00:46:47,820 --> 00:46:49,780
Like nobody cares what I think or say

169
00:46:49,780 --> 00:46:52,360
with my Nobel Prize and my fame.

170
00:46:52,760 --> 00:46:56,400
So he's been trying to push how there's path dependence

171
00:46:56,400 --> 00:46:57,000
in location.

172
00:46:57,000 --> 00:46:59,460
Like why is it that so much carpet is made

173
00:46:59,740 --> 00:47:01,980
in this one area outside of Atlanta.

174
00:47:01,980 --> 00:47:04,100
And again his story which makes a lot

175
00:47:04,100 --> 00:47:07,260
of sense is people want to build their carpet factory there

176
00:47:07,260 --> 00:47:09,020
because all the other carpet factories are there.

177
00:47:09,020 --> 00:47:11,480
So all the workers that know how to do the carpet are there.

178
00:47:11,780 --> 00:47:13,520
And all the input suppliers are there.

179
00:47:13,520 --> 00:47:16,160
So once it's there then you want to stay there.

180
00:47:16,160 --> 00:47:17,980
Like why the diamond industry in Manhattan.

181
00:47:18,340 --> 00:47:19,400
There's a bunch of obvious reasons

182
00:47:19,400 --> 00:47:21,080
to not have a diamond industry in Manhattan.

183
00:47:22,160 --> 00:47:25,440
In fact it's pretty much the same reason like for almost any

184
00:47:25,440 --> 00:47:27,180
kind of manufacturing there's an obvious reason

185
00:47:27,180 --> 00:47:28,160
to not have it in Manhattan.

186
00:47:29,160 --> 00:47:31,860
Right? Like what's the obvious reason

187
00:47:31,860 --> 00:47:32,720
not to be in Manhattan?

188
00:47:32,720 --> 00:47:36,020
Yes. Like a really high cost of doing business there.

189
00:47:36,500 --> 00:47:38,980
So like the only thing you'd really want to have

190
00:47:38,980 --> 00:47:40,820
in Manhattan when you think about it is something involving

191
00:47:40,820 --> 00:47:41,480
the tourists.

192
00:47:41,880 --> 00:47:44,860
Something involving like actually there's a lot

193
00:47:44,860 --> 00:47:47,320
of high population density is what makes the product good.

194
00:47:47,320 --> 00:47:49,940
But for manufacturing it's going to get dispersed

195
00:47:49,940 --> 00:47:50,860
over the whole country.

196
00:47:51,140 --> 00:47:52,240
Over the whole world even.

197
00:47:52,560 --> 00:47:54,120
So it's hard to see what the point is.

198
00:47:54,120 --> 00:47:55,560
Then there's an obvious disadvantage.

199
00:47:55,560 --> 00:47:58,380
But still as long as you have the other people

200
00:47:58,380 --> 00:48:00,180
that are doing it there then you want to be there too.

201
00:48:00,180 --> 00:48:01,900
Same with the stock market obviously.

202
00:48:02,300 --> 00:48:03,220
All right.

203
00:48:03,760 --> 00:48:07,160
And then for many other regional things like why have all

204
00:48:07,160 --> 00:48:10,860
of you, why have so much of the tech industry in the Bay Area?

205
00:48:11,480 --> 00:48:13,600
Which again has the same problems as Manhattan.

206
00:48:13,600 --> 00:48:15,840
It's a terrible place for most purposes.

207
00:48:16,200 --> 00:48:18,720
Like why make a lot of people move to a place

208
00:48:18,980 --> 00:48:21,260
where a million dollars a year isn't even a good salary

209
00:48:21,260 --> 00:48:21,880
anymore?

210
00:48:21,880 --> 00:48:24,220
It seems like a dumb thing to do.

211
00:48:24,560 --> 00:48:27,600
But again it looks like there is this lock in that you get

212
00:48:27,600 --> 00:48:29,680
where once other people are doing it you want to do it too.

213
00:48:29,680 --> 00:48:32,500
Even though it seems like there would be a way better way

214
00:48:32,500 --> 00:48:32,940
of doing it.

215
00:48:33,120 --> 00:48:34,100
Is there a question here?

216
00:48:34,180 --> 00:48:34,280
Yeah.

217
00:48:34,280 --> 00:48:34,380
Yeah.

218
00:48:34,380 --> 00:48:36,140
Is the Diamond Locket in New York partially,

219
00:48:36,140 --> 00:48:37,980
is it like a cultural example too almost I guess?

220
00:48:37,980 --> 00:48:39,660
Is that primarily like a lot of Jewish people?

221
00:48:39,660 --> 00:48:39,960
Yeah, yeah.

222
00:48:39,960 --> 00:48:41,680
So a lot of Hasidic Jews are involved.

223
00:48:41,680 --> 00:48:45,280
So although they too they have no actual religious reason

224
00:48:45,280 --> 00:48:48,260
to be there other than the fact there's other Hasidic Jews there.

225
00:48:48,680 --> 00:48:51,680
So they can all coordinate to just go and move to Florida.

226
00:48:52,360 --> 00:48:55,400
So it's one thing if you,

227
00:48:55,400 --> 00:48:57,600
if the religion actually specifies that Jerusalem is the

228
00:48:57,600 --> 00:49:00,220
holy city but as far as I know there's no doctrine

229
00:49:00,220 --> 00:49:02,240
about Manhattan being a holy city or anything.

230
00:49:02,240 --> 00:49:06,500
So if you could just get the Hasidic Jews to go and move

231
00:49:06,500 --> 00:49:10,220
to a lower rent place seems like a big improvement for them.

232
00:49:11,300 --> 00:49:11,620
All right.

233
00:49:12,520 --> 00:49:15,440
Now normally when people are talking

234
00:49:15,440 --> 00:49:17,300
about technology they use different jargon.

235
00:49:17,880 --> 00:49:19,880
So the preferred jargon is path dependence.

236
00:49:19,880 --> 00:49:23,740
All right.

237
00:49:23,740 --> 00:49:27,780
And under this name which is just another name

238
00:49:27,780 --> 00:49:28,900
for there being a coordination,

239
00:49:28,900 --> 00:49:30,760
coordination problems something like that.

240
00:49:30,760 --> 00:49:33,140
So there's a bunch of economists who pointed

241
00:49:33,140 --> 00:49:35,120
to various alleged examples

242
00:49:35,120 --> 00:49:36,620
of inefficient technological lock-in.

243
00:49:37,460 --> 00:49:40,240
And as I mentioned QWERTY is still probably the most

244
00:49:40,240 --> 00:49:41,100
famous example.

245
00:49:41,440 --> 00:49:44,260
So again just imagine typing the second row

246
00:49:44,260 --> 00:49:48,780
on your keyboard the non-word QWERTY, Q-W-E-R-T-Y.

247
00:49:48,780 --> 00:49:52,140
All right so that has been around for a really long time.

248
00:49:53,960 --> 00:49:57,500
So and there is a story that sounds pretty plausible

249
00:49:57,500 --> 00:49:58,360
on the surface saying look,

250
00:49:58,780 --> 00:50:03,380
the reason why we have this keyboard is it's just a hold

251
00:50:03,380 --> 00:50:06,080
over from earlier times when there were actually physical

252
00:50:06,080 --> 00:50:09,140
keys and you need to actually slow type this down in order

253
00:50:09,140 --> 00:50:10,520
to avoid jamming of the keys.

254
00:50:11,060 --> 00:50:12,080
This is the standard one.

255
00:50:12,080 --> 00:50:13,840
I don't know if you were anything like me

256
00:50:13,840 --> 00:50:14,600
when you first learned to type.

257
00:50:14,600 --> 00:50:16,620
Did you ever ask someone why is it just alphabetical?

258
00:50:16,620 --> 00:50:20,940
That was my, that seemed like an obvious question.

259
00:50:20,940 --> 00:50:24,860
And I think I did get, so see I think my mom had,

260
00:50:24,860 --> 00:50:27,560
yeah my mom had actually been a professional secretary

261
00:50:27,560 --> 00:50:28,800
in the 40s and 50s.

262
00:50:29,360 --> 00:50:31,960
And she actually had a different story.

263
00:50:31,960 --> 00:50:33,500
Her story was that you want to have keys

264
00:50:33,500 --> 00:50:35,820
that are frequently typed right next to each other.

265
00:50:36,300 --> 00:50:39,040
So it was, it was the, it was not actually just deliberately

266
00:50:39,040 --> 00:50:40,500
slow down the typing to avoid jamming.

267
00:50:41,180 --> 00:50:44,500
But anyway, but when it was another story based upon there

268
00:50:44,500 --> 00:50:46,900
being physical, actually that one, that wasn't really

269
00:50:46,900 --> 00:50:48,560
about the keys because that one was still all over.

270
00:50:48,560 --> 00:50:52,480
So, anyway, important thing to remember

271
00:50:52,480 --> 00:50:56,180
about all these models is that it's not,

272
00:50:56,180 --> 00:50:58,580
these models are not like the prisoner's dilemma at all.

273
00:50:59,040 --> 00:51:01,160
In the prisoner's dilemma there's actually a story

274
00:51:01,160 --> 00:51:05,860
about why society naturally goes to the Pareto inferior equilibrium

275
00:51:05,860 --> 00:51:07,740
or the Caldex inferior equilibrium.

276
00:51:08,420 --> 00:51:10,580
You look over here, is there any particular reason to think

277
00:51:10,580 --> 00:51:13,260
that you're going to go to the lower payoff equilibrium?

278
00:51:13,260 --> 00:51:15,340
Not at all.

279
00:51:15,500 --> 00:51:18,440
In fact, there is a good reason to think that you won't.

280
00:51:19,260 --> 00:51:21,320
Right? There's a lot of reasons to think that you won't.

281
00:51:21,740 --> 00:51:23,120
Just starting with Thomas Schelling.

282
00:51:24,120 --> 00:51:25,920
Remember, so if you were playing this game

283
00:51:25,920 --> 00:51:27,800
with a randomly selected other human being on earth,

284
00:51:28,360 --> 00:51:30,160
like can you imagine not playing right?

285
00:51:30,160 --> 00:51:32,600
It would be crazy not to play right.

286
00:51:33,160 --> 00:51:35,000
Right? You say, well, of course you would play right.

287
00:51:35,360 --> 00:51:37,500
And if the other person played left and you said why,

288
00:51:37,500 --> 00:51:38,640
is it because I thought you would play left,

289
00:51:38,640 --> 00:51:39,940
well why would you think I would do that?

290
00:51:39,940 --> 00:51:41,780
It's like, I don't know, I just thought you would.

291
00:51:41,780 --> 00:51:43,480
Right? All right.

292
00:51:43,480 --> 00:51:46,680
So there's something very psychologically appealing

293
00:51:46,680 --> 00:51:50,120
about the outcome that is better for both parties.

294
00:51:51,040 --> 00:51:54,760
Now if you had one that was more like this, let's see,

295
00:51:54,760 --> 00:51:56,160
so which one, let's see.

296
00:51:56,160 --> 00:52:02,040
So you had one that was like 5, 3, and 3, 5.

297
00:52:02,300 --> 00:52:03,300
That's one where you can start

298
00:52:03,300 --> 00:52:05,900
to more easily understand why people might be doing different

299
00:52:05,900 --> 00:52:08,960
things because the two sides might disagree

300
00:52:08,960 --> 00:52:09,880
about which one is better.

301
00:52:09,880 --> 00:52:13,000
And they might even, for public relations, say, you know,

302
00:52:13,000 --> 00:52:14,940
this guy might say, oh, left is better for society,

303
00:52:14,940 --> 00:52:16,440
this guy says right is better for society,

304
00:52:16,680 --> 00:52:18,320
but it's actually a disagreement

305
00:52:18,320 --> 00:52:19,920
about whose interests should prevail.

306
00:52:20,480 --> 00:52:22,120
So anyway, you can imagine that happening.

307
00:52:22,980 --> 00:52:23,300
All right.

308
00:52:24,320 --> 00:52:24,780
All right.

309
00:52:25,520 --> 00:52:27,120
All right.

310
00:52:27,120 --> 00:52:29,220
So anyway, but, so, you know,

311
00:52:29,220 --> 00:52:31,120
the key thing about these coordination games is it's only

312
00:52:31,120 --> 00:52:33,560
possible that you would get locked into the inferior one.

313
00:52:33,560 --> 00:52:33,860
Yes?

314
00:52:33,860 --> 00:52:34,460
Yeah, Nathan.

315
00:52:34,460 --> 00:52:39,900
Could it be possible that there are situations

316
00:52:39,900 --> 00:52:41,960
where people are ignorant of the specific payoffs but know

317
00:52:41,960 --> 00:52:43,800
that coordination has a positive payoff?

318
00:52:43,800 --> 00:52:44,200
Yeah, sure.

319
00:52:44,200 --> 00:52:46,400
Payoffs are expressed as like a complicated equation.

320
00:52:46,400 --> 00:52:48,340
Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly.

321
00:52:48,340 --> 00:52:50,000
So that would be a case.

322
00:52:50,240 --> 00:52:51,000
Or how about just one

323
00:52:51,000 --> 00:52:52,800
where the information gets revealed very gradually

324
00:52:52,800 --> 00:52:53,700
and there's a lot of players.

325
00:52:53,900 --> 00:52:56,680
So then, you know, suppose I'm trying 1 million,

326
00:52:56,680 --> 00:52:59,040
it becomes, it's revealed that right is actually better.

327
00:53:00,080 --> 00:53:02,960
And then just player number 1,000,001 want to go

328
00:53:02,960 --> 00:53:03,760
and switch over.

329
00:53:03,760 --> 00:53:05,280
Yeah, he's probably going to say, well,

330
00:53:05,280 --> 00:53:06,640
like other people are going to be doing,

331
00:53:06,640 --> 00:53:08,320
are going to keep doing what they've always been doing.

332
00:53:08,320 --> 00:53:09,880
So I'm not going to be the one to switch over.

333
00:53:10,300 --> 00:53:11,720
Just like now it's obvious

334
00:53:11,720 --> 00:53:13,820
that English is an inferior language to Esperanto.

335
00:53:14,320 --> 00:53:15,660
Do you want to be the person

336
00:53:15,660 --> 00:53:17,760
that starts speaking Esperanto all the time?

337
00:53:18,280 --> 00:53:19,660
Right? Probably not.

338
00:53:20,260 --> 00:53:22,640
There is a William Shatner movie on Esperanto.

339
00:53:22,640 --> 00:53:26,580
I'm not sure why he did that.

340
00:53:26,580 --> 00:53:30,240
But I saw a couple minutes that looked terrible on that level.

341
00:53:30,240 --> 00:53:34,880
But even by the Shatner standards.

342
00:53:35,280 --> 00:53:35,540
All right.

343
00:53:35,880 --> 00:53:37,880
So anyway, inefficient lock-in is possible.

344
00:53:38,280 --> 00:53:40,280
But there's always another possibility,

345
00:53:40,280 --> 00:53:42,200
which is the status quo is actually optimal.

346
00:53:42,480 --> 00:53:45,600
And the complaining you hear is what we call special pleading,

347
00:53:45,840 --> 00:53:48,420
where there's some people who are losing out or just trying

348
00:53:48,420 --> 00:53:51,480
to get attention, and they don't end, they're actually wrong.

349
00:53:52,560 --> 00:53:54,580
Okay? And then there's another possibility,

350
00:53:54,580 --> 00:53:56,480
which is the implausible for something like language,

351
00:53:56,880 --> 00:53:58,400
that if we would be better off today

352
00:53:58,400 --> 00:54:00,480
if we had all chosen a different language long ago,

353
00:54:00,700 --> 00:54:01,880
but it's not worth switching anymore.

354
00:54:03,160 --> 00:54:03,260
All right.

355
00:54:03,260 --> 00:54:05,600
So knowing what we know now, sure it would be better

356
00:54:05,600 --> 00:54:06,840
if we could go and rewind.

357
00:54:07,300 --> 00:54:09,560
But we can't rewind, and it's just not worth doing.

358
00:54:10,120 --> 00:54:13,700
Right? And so the QWERTY example specifically,

359
00:54:14,040 --> 00:54:17,780
there are several very well-known papers

360
00:54:17,780 --> 00:54:20,040
by McGraw and Leibovitz where they debunk the QWERTY example.

361
00:54:20,800 --> 00:54:22,660
It is one where it is pretty hard

362
00:54:22,660 --> 00:54:25,540
to understand what the coordination story is anymore.

363
00:54:25,540 --> 00:54:28,400
Because now it is really easy to convert any keyboard

364
00:54:28,400 --> 00:54:30,000
into a non-QWERTY keyboard.

365
00:54:31,500 --> 00:54:32,560
So is anyone here trying?

366
00:54:32,560 --> 00:54:33,640
I think I already asked you.

367
00:54:33,640 --> 00:54:34,320
Like one person.

368
00:54:34,320 --> 00:54:36,060
Has anyone ever tried switching?

369
00:54:36,820 --> 00:54:37,200
Like, you know.

370
00:54:37,760 --> 00:54:39,440
Like, was there anyone who when you were first learning

371
00:54:39,440 --> 00:54:41,600
to type decided you wanted to try doing something different?

372
00:54:42,080 --> 00:54:43,460
Like experiment, let's try a few.

373
00:54:43,460 --> 00:54:44,700
And what did you learn?

374
00:54:45,340 --> 00:54:48,620
When I was in elementary school, we had a typing class,

375
00:54:48,620 --> 00:54:49,940
and we tried some Du Bois stuff.

376
00:54:50,780 --> 00:54:51,500
And which one?

377
00:54:51,500 --> 00:54:52,500
And did you like it better?

378
00:54:52,500 --> 00:54:53,900
No. It's harder.

379
00:54:54,340 --> 00:54:54,820
It is harder.

380
00:54:54,820 --> 00:54:58,000
I'd have to think that some European keyboards would be

381
00:54:58,000 --> 00:54:59,060
completely different from ours.

382
00:54:59,580 --> 00:55:00,160
So let's see.

383
00:55:00,160 --> 00:55:01,340
So I was in Germany this summer.

384
00:55:01,340 --> 00:55:03,780
So the German keyboard is very similar to the U.S. one,

385
00:55:03,780 --> 00:55:06,560
but they do have separate keys for the umlaut letters

386
00:55:06,560 --> 00:55:07,280
and things like that.

387
00:55:07,280 --> 00:55:08,380
Yeah, like that or like that.

388
00:55:08,380 --> 00:55:09,860
And then they move a couple other ones.

389
00:55:09,860 --> 00:55:11,260
So like the Z is in a different place

390
00:55:11,260 --> 00:55:12,580
because they use the Z all the time.

391
00:55:13,260 --> 00:55:16,220
But still it's almost the same as the U.S. one.

392
00:55:16,600 --> 00:55:18,200
Although you just adjust it, I mean, just enough.

393
00:55:18,200 --> 00:55:20,780
And actually, you know, so and there they set it up

394
00:55:20,780 --> 00:55:22,780
so that you just go and flip a switch

395
00:55:22,780 --> 00:55:24,220
and it becomes a regular American keyboard.

396
00:55:24,220 --> 00:55:26,680
What about something like a Russian or Hungarian?

397
00:55:26,880 --> 00:55:28,480
Yes. So it's totally different alphabet

398
00:55:28,480 --> 00:55:29,280
that I don't know whether it is.

399
00:55:29,280 --> 00:55:32,160
But they're not doing QWERTY in an alphabet

400
00:55:32,160 --> 00:55:33,560
that doesn't have the same letters.

401
00:55:33,560 --> 00:55:36,600
I guess you could try to mimic it as closely as possible.

402
00:55:36,840 --> 00:55:37,140
Right.

403
00:55:37,760 --> 00:55:42,080
Right. But in any case, you know, like as the cost

404
00:55:42,080 --> 00:55:44,800
of being the one person doing something different goes down,

405
00:55:44,800 --> 00:55:46,560
if you don't see much change in behavior,

406
00:55:46,920 --> 00:55:49,940
suggests that at least the simple coordination story is

407
00:55:49,940 --> 00:55:54,680
wrong and it's actually something else going on.

408
00:55:54,680 --> 00:55:54,780
All right.

409
00:55:55,120 --> 00:55:58,200
So now these coordination problems seem especially

410
00:55:58,200 --> 00:56:00,040
unlikely when the number of players is small.

411
00:56:00,480 --> 00:56:03,980
So yes, you know, if you had the original payoffs,

412
00:56:04,460 --> 00:56:09,520
3-3, 5-5, you know, if we only had five players

413
00:56:09,520 --> 00:56:12,200
and we were there, then again, it seems pretty easy,

414
00:56:12,200 --> 00:56:13,520
it might be pretty easy to switch over.

415
00:56:14,440 --> 00:56:16,120
Right. Five people just get together and say,

416
00:56:16,120 --> 00:56:17,940
come on people, like we all know this is better.

417
00:56:17,940 --> 00:56:18,760
Let's just do it for now.

418
00:56:18,760 --> 00:56:20,020
Let's just do it.

419
00:56:20,020 --> 00:56:23,960
And notice that this is very different from say the members

420
00:56:23,960 --> 00:56:25,900
of OPEC all agreeing to cut back the oil production.

421
00:56:27,040 --> 00:56:28,480
Right. Because if all the other members

422
00:56:28,480 --> 00:56:29,820
of OPEC cut back the oil production,

423
00:56:29,820 --> 00:56:30,580
what do you want to do?

424
00:56:30,580 --> 00:56:33,580
Yeah, in fact, you want to increase your oil production.

425
00:56:33,860 --> 00:56:36,120
On the other hand, if everybody but you switches

426
00:56:36,120 --> 00:56:38,800
to blank right, do you want to go to ha, ha,

427
00:56:38,800 --> 00:56:41,220
suckers all driving the left-hand side of the road?

428
00:56:41,220 --> 00:56:43,800
And then you want to switch over too.

429
00:56:43,800 --> 00:56:47,300
Right. Which is part of an argument that I had

430
00:56:47,300 --> 00:56:52,180
with Tyler Cowen years ago about the sustainability anarchism,

431
00:56:52,180 --> 00:56:54,120
but we won't get into that here.

432
00:56:56,740 --> 00:56:57,100
All right.

433
00:56:57,100 --> 00:56:59,600
So anyway, so coordination problems are pretty unlikely

434
00:56:59,600 --> 00:57:00,980
when the number of players is small.

435
00:57:01,280 --> 00:57:05,440
Or another way that you can get to the better outcome is

436
00:57:05,440 --> 00:57:06,440
if you have market leaders.

437
00:57:06,440 --> 00:57:08,640
You can have a lot of firms, but if there are a few firms

438
00:57:08,640 --> 00:57:10,440
that are focal for people paying attention to them,

439
00:57:11,080 --> 00:57:14,120
then you can, then once again, there is a very easy way

440
00:57:14,120 --> 00:57:16,080
of getting out of it, which is the focal leaders say,

441
00:57:16,080 --> 00:57:18,140
we're going to switch, and then everybody else switches

442
00:57:18,140 --> 00:57:18,740
behind them.

443
00:57:19,080 --> 00:57:21,280
So Walmart seemed to be focal in the market

444
00:57:21,320 --> 00:57:23,800
for a high-definition DVD player.

445
00:57:24,300 --> 00:57:26,600
So not HD DVD, which is a trademark

446
00:57:26,600 --> 00:57:29,200
for a specific technology, but the general concept

447
00:57:29,660 --> 00:57:32,560
of a high-definition disc that you would play.

448
00:57:33,140 --> 00:57:34,920
Right. And you've probably heard the story

449
00:57:34,920 --> 00:57:38,080
about how the US time zones were originally selected.

450
00:57:38,880 --> 00:57:42,020
So which company, which market leaders had a big stake

451
00:57:42,020 --> 00:57:42,960
and worked something out?

452
00:57:43,840 --> 00:57:44,660
Railroads.

453
00:57:44,660 --> 00:57:48,000
Railroads. So daylight savings time, that was government,

454
00:57:48,120 --> 00:57:51,300
but the time zones themselves were done by railroads.

455
00:57:52,180 --> 00:57:55,820
Right. I still remember the day that Windows 95 was released,

456
00:57:56,360 --> 00:57:59,640
where Bill Gates himself came out and bought a ton

457
00:57:59,640 --> 00:58:01,860
of network television to go and tell everybody,

458
00:58:01,860 --> 00:58:04,320
now we're all switching over to Windows 95, people.

459
00:58:04,320 --> 00:58:05,260
All right.

460
00:58:05,260 --> 00:58:07,160
So it was already like two years late,

461
00:58:07,220 --> 00:58:09,080
but that's why it was called Windows 95.

462
00:58:09,700 --> 00:58:10,400
All right.

463
00:58:10,400 --> 00:58:12,420
So this is another way.

464
00:58:12,420 --> 00:58:14,700
And you can imagine what would have happened

465
00:58:14,700 --> 00:58:17,360
if there had not been any market leader in operating systems.

466
00:58:17,800 --> 00:58:19,960
Of course, there's a lot of complaining about Microsoft,

467
00:58:20,660 --> 00:58:23,340
but it is worth considering

468
00:58:23,600 --> 00:58:25,600
that there might be something worse than Microsoft.

469
00:58:26,180 --> 00:58:27,700
Like there could be chaos.

470
00:58:28,240 --> 00:58:30,040
There could be 20 different operating systems,

471
00:58:30,040 --> 00:58:34,740
every one of which is inherently superior to Windows,

472
00:58:35,080 --> 00:58:38,260
and yet they are all mutually compatible with each other,

473
00:58:38,260 --> 00:58:40,180
and you have to either learn 20 operating systems

474
00:58:40,180 --> 00:58:43,260
or just deal with the incompatibility.

475
00:58:43,740 --> 00:58:45,120
I still remember back

476
00:58:45,240 --> 00:58:47,240
when email systems were not really compatible.

477
00:58:47,240 --> 00:58:51,280
So in the early years of email, like 1989, 90,

478
00:58:51,540 --> 00:58:54,220
if you emailed something to a person at another university,

479
00:58:54,700 --> 00:58:56,860
never mind a non-academic server,

480
00:58:57,100 --> 00:58:59,100
there's about a three-quarters bounce rate.

481
00:59:00,000 --> 00:59:01,740
And just said, cannot be interpreted.

482
00:59:01,740 --> 00:59:02,780
I don't know.

483
00:59:02,780 --> 00:59:05,260
So and then if you tried emailing a private thing,

484
00:59:05,260 --> 00:59:09,220
like CompuServe or AOL, then like it almost never worked.

485
00:59:09,220 --> 00:59:12,340
So and then there are all kinds of other problems,

486
00:59:12,340 --> 00:59:13,820
but in backspace and things like that.

487
00:59:13,820 --> 00:59:15,840
But anyway, so but you know,

488
00:59:15,840 --> 00:59:17,380
you have all these mutual incompatibilities

489
00:59:17,380 --> 00:59:20,840
between the systems, and it seems clearly superior

490
00:59:20,840 --> 00:59:23,660
to have one system, even if that system isn't that great,

491
00:59:24,520 --> 00:59:26,500
than to having a bunch of incompatible systems.

492
00:59:27,460 --> 00:59:27,640
All right.

493
00:59:28,200 --> 00:59:30,460
So again, my guess is that without,

494
00:59:30,460 --> 00:59:32,800
if there had been no focal leader in operating systems,

495
00:59:33,340 --> 00:59:35,360
that people wouldn't be complaining

496
