58
01:22:07,800 --> 01:22:09,880
All right, so there is that.

59
01:22:11,140 --> 01:22:12,880
All right, now, just to have a little digression

60
01:22:12,880 --> 01:22:14,420
into history, thought, and philosophy.

61
01:22:14,420 --> 01:22:18,080
So how many people ever read Thomas Hobbes' Leviathan?

62
01:22:18,080 --> 01:22:19,360
All right, and remember,

63
01:22:19,360 --> 01:22:22,420
what are the five bad things about the state of nature?

64
01:22:22,420 --> 01:22:26,700
Solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short, yes.

65
01:22:26,700 --> 01:22:31,700
Like the five dwarves of the Hobbesian state of nature.

66
01:22:32,300 --> 01:22:35,460
Solitary, yes, poor, solitary, nasty, brutish, and short.

67
01:22:35,460 --> 01:22:38,020
All right, now, if you go back and read

68
01:22:38,020 --> 01:22:41,340
exactly what Hobbes' argument is,

69
01:22:41,340 --> 01:22:45,100
it's very striking once you understand some game theory

70
01:22:45,100 --> 01:22:47,420
because there's an overpowering temptation

71
01:22:47,420 --> 01:22:49,780
that people who know game theory have

72
01:22:49,780 --> 01:22:52,860
to interpret Thomas Hobbes as if he was right.

73
01:22:52,860 --> 01:22:55,580
But if you actually read his own words,

74
01:22:55,580 --> 01:22:57,540
I maintain that you will go and say,

75
01:22:57,540 --> 01:22:59,940
this guy doesn't know what he's talking about.

76
01:22:59,940 --> 01:23:03,380
A very smart guy who just didn't even understand

77
01:23:03,380 --> 01:23:07,140
the basics of what we now understand based upon this class.

78
01:23:07,140 --> 01:23:08,300
Right, and why would I say this?

79
01:23:08,300 --> 01:23:09,740
Well, so go and read it.

80
01:23:09,740 --> 01:23:11,340
And again, don't read it with the presumption

81
01:23:11,340 --> 01:23:12,160
that it's right.

82
01:23:12,160 --> 01:23:14,220
Just read it, read the literal words

83
01:23:14,220 --> 01:23:17,500
in the most natural, ordinary interpretation.

84
01:23:17,500 --> 01:23:20,060
So here's what I claim Hobbes literally says.

85
01:23:21,060 --> 01:23:23,140
He seems to literally say

86
01:23:23,140 --> 01:23:26,380
that war is a strictly dominant strategy.

87
01:23:26,380 --> 01:23:29,380
Right, and you're always better off fighting.

88
01:23:29,380 --> 01:23:31,300
Why are you always better off fighting?

89
01:23:31,300 --> 01:23:32,900
Yeah, actually, it's a story.

90
01:23:32,900 --> 01:23:34,020
And it says war.

91
01:23:34,020 --> 01:23:36,100
So either the other side was gonna fight anyway,

92
01:23:36,100 --> 01:23:37,740
in which case, prepared.

93
01:23:37,740 --> 01:23:41,020
Or you went, or you couldn't be sure.

94
01:23:41,020 --> 01:23:42,140
And if you can't be sure,

95
01:23:42,140 --> 01:23:44,300
then you should just go and kill people, right?

96
01:23:44,300 --> 01:23:45,580
That makes perfect sense.

97
01:23:45,580 --> 01:23:48,620
Right, and again, if you read it,

98
01:23:48,620 --> 01:23:51,740
he does describe that it will degenerate

99
01:23:51,740 --> 01:23:53,380
into universal warfare.

100
01:23:53,380 --> 01:23:57,500
But the idea that there is any downside at all of fighting

101
01:23:57,500 --> 01:23:59,680
seems completely outside of his thinking.

102
01:24:00,860 --> 01:24:03,120
The idea that you might go and get killed

103
01:24:03,120 --> 01:24:04,540
because you picked a fight with someone

104
01:24:04,540 --> 01:24:08,260
that wasn't gonna fight you, totally absent.

105
01:24:08,260 --> 01:24:10,340
The idea that you might have just peacefully cooperated

106
01:24:10,340 --> 01:24:12,340
with someone, totally absent.

107
01:24:12,340 --> 01:24:14,880
Instead, there is this very strong assumption

108
01:24:14,880 --> 01:24:16,940
that whoever fights wins.

109
01:24:16,940 --> 01:24:19,140
And even if both sides are fighting,

110
01:24:19,140 --> 01:24:24,140
better to come and fight than to just be sitting there.

111
01:24:26,120 --> 01:24:28,540
So either the other person is not gonna fight,

112
01:24:28,540 --> 01:24:30,580
in which case, hit him over the head with a club.

113
01:24:30,580 --> 01:24:32,060
And or if the guy's got a club,

114
01:24:32,060 --> 01:24:33,940
hit him over the head with a club.

115
01:24:33,940 --> 01:24:35,980
Right, which, the way I get it,

116
01:24:35,980 --> 01:24:37,340
remember, he is talking about,

117
01:24:37,340 --> 01:24:39,620
yeah, he's specifically talking about

118
01:24:39,620 --> 01:24:41,500
two random individuals beating each other

119
01:24:41,500 --> 01:24:42,660
in the state of nature.

120
01:24:42,660 --> 01:24:45,900
All right, now just think about this.

121
01:24:45,900 --> 01:24:49,780
Suppose that you are on a desert island all by yourself

122
01:24:49,780 --> 01:24:51,900
and another person washes up.

123
01:24:51,900 --> 01:24:54,140
How, what would have to happen

124
01:24:54,140 --> 01:24:56,060
before you would consider it a good strategy

125
01:24:56,060 --> 01:24:57,420
to go and murder that person

126
01:24:57,420 --> 01:24:59,580
before they are able to go and cough the water

127
01:24:59,580 --> 01:25:00,620
up out of their lungs?

128
01:25:00,620 --> 01:25:02,020
How big are they?

129
01:25:02,020 --> 01:25:04,220
That is a consideration.

130
01:25:04,220 --> 01:25:05,780
All right, so suppose the person's like

131
01:25:05,780 --> 01:25:07,220
two feet bigger than you.

132
01:25:07,220 --> 01:25:09,620
Are you still inclined to try to murder that person

133
01:25:09,620 --> 01:25:10,980
when you're all alone by yourself?

134
01:25:10,980 --> 01:25:11,980
Probably not.

135
01:25:11,980 --> 01:25:13,140
Yes.

136
01:25:13,140 --> 01:25:17,220
And see, again, that seems like a very poor strategy.

137
01:25:17,220 --> 01:25:19,340
Again, not that, well, how do you know

138
01:25:19,340 --> 01:25:20,500
he isn't a Charles Manson?

139
01:25:20,500 --> 01:25:22,540
I'm like a seven foot tall Charles Manson.

140
01:25:22,540 --> 01:25:23,700
All right, I don't.

141
01:25:23,700 --> 01:25:26,260
But it seems unlikely.

142
01:25:26,260 --> 01:25:28,940
And if I do, and if he wasn't Charles Manson,

143
01:25:28,940 --> 01:25:30,660
I murdered him, then I have to be,

144
01:25:30,660 --> 01:25:33,400
I have to continue being alone by myself.

145
01:25:33,400 --> 01:25:34,740
I'm like really lonely.

146
01:25:34,740 --> 01:25:36,100
I have no one to help me with anything.

147
01:25:36,100 --> 01:25:38,540
If I ever get sick, there's no one to bail me out.

148
01:25:38,540 --> 01:25:41,060
I'm in a terrible situation.

149
01:25:41,060 --> 01:25:43,780
And obviously, no one would think this

150
01:25:43,780 --> 01:25:45,540
if it were opposite genders.

151
01:25:46,460 --> 01:25:47,460
So if we're opposite genders,

152
01:25:47,460 --> 01:25:49,020
there's a man on an island, a woman washes up,

153
01:25:49,020 --> 01:25:50,700
no one thinks that the obvious prediction

154
01:25:50,700 --> 01:25:53,020
is he immediately murders the person and shuts me out.

155
01:25:53,020 --> 01:25:54,820
Or vice versa.

156
01:25:54,820 --> 01:25:56,700
No one thinks that a woman sees a man

157
01:25:56,700 --> 01:25:57,820
and washes him off the shore.

158
01:25:57,820 --> 01:25:59,380
All right, better kill him.

159
01:25:59,380 --> 01:26:00,700
See, it's the dominant strategy

160
01:26:00,700 --> 01:26:02,260
according to Hobbes' Leviathan.

161
01:26:02,260 --> 01:26:05,540
And then when you start thinking like,

162
01:26:05,540 --> 01:26:07,420
how bad would the situation have to be

163
01:26:07,420 --> 01:26:10,740
before it would be plausible that you would predict

164
01:26:10,740 --> 01:26:12,620
that the person would go and murder the,

165
01:26:12,620 --> 01:26:13,700
like the person who's already there

166
01:26:13,700 --> 01:26:15,780
would immediately murder the newcomer.

167
01:26:15,780 --> 01:26:18,020
So maybe if it's World War II

168
01:26:18,020 --> 01:26:19,420
and you're an American on the island

169
01:26:19,420 --> 01:26:21,460
and a Japanese soldier washes up,

170
01:26:22,940 --> 01:26:24,940
right, maybe then you kill him.

171
01:26:24,940 --> 01:26:27,020
Although I say even that depends on how many days

172
01:26:27,020 --> 01:26:29,460
you've been alone on that island.

173
01:26:29,460 --> 01:26:31,580
Like a week, yeah, you probably will kill him,

174
01:26:31,580 --> 01:26:33,940
especially if you've already been in combat

175
01:26:33,940 --> 01:26:36,700
and you've seen some friends get killed by Japanese soldiers.

176
01:26:36,700 --> 01:26:40,700
Then murdering him before he does anything is plausible,

177
01:26:40,700 --> 01:26:42,700
but you've been alone for a year,

178
01:26:43,540 --> 01:26:45,980
how are you gonna murder this guy?

179
01:26:45,980 --> 01:26:48,300
And it starts to seem highly implausible

180
01:26:48,300 --> 01:26:50,420
that this would happen, right?

181
01:26:50,420 --> 01:26:53,580
And again, partly it may just be that

182
01:26:53,580 --> 01:26:55,580
there's some psychology that Hobbes is considering,

183
01:26:55,580 --> 01:26:57,700
but again, there's also the obvious one of,

184
01:26:57,700 --> 01:27:00,020
look, there's a chance the person's gonna try to kill me,

185
01:27:00,020 --> 01:27:02,060
but there's also enormous gains of trade

186
01:27:02,060 --> 01:27:04,180
when you're the only person around.

187
01:27:04,180 --> 01:27:07,620
And if I kill a person, yes, I won't risk anything,

188
01:27:07,620 --> 01:27:11,380
but I also will give up all the possible gains,

189
01:27:11,380 --> 01:27:14,460
which in expected value terms are enormous.

190
01:27:15,620 --> 01:27:19,300
So that's something very weird about the scenario.

191
01:27:20,380 --> 01:27:23,020
Right, and then on top of all this,

192
01:27:23,020 --> 01:27:26,020
there's something else that isn't quite reflected there,

193
01:27:26,020 --> 01:27:29,100
but suppose that you go and decide

194
01:27:29,100 --> 01:27:32,620
you're going to fight a stranger right now and you win.

195
01:27:32,620 --> 01:27:35,620
Does that mean that everything worked out well for you

196
01:27:35,620 --> 01:27:38,820
just because you kill another person in the fight?

197
01:27:38,820 --> 01:27:42,180
Well, very often you get injured, right?

198
01:27:42,180 --> 01:27:43,700
So suppose that in that case,

199
01:27:43,700 --> 01:27:46,260
even if all you care about is physical safety,

200
01:27:46,260 --> 01:27:47,700
there is a since possibility

201
01:27:47,700 --> 01:27:49,100
that when you fight another person,

202
01:27:49,100 --> 01:27:51,820
that even if you win, you will be injured

203
01:27:51,820 --> 01:27:54,580
and the loss of the injury will outweigh

204
01:27:54,580 --> 01:27:57,020
whatever risk that the other person posed to you.

205
01:27:58,260 --> 01:28:00,100
Right, in fact, you could get mortally wounded

206
01:28:00,100 --> 01:28:01,220
even though you won the fight.

207
01:28:01,220 --> 01:28:03,100
That's a very extreme case.

208
01:28:03,100 --> 01:28:04,620
But again, imagine that you go,

209
01:28:04,620 --> 01:28:06,580
you could go and lose a finger.

210
01:28:06,580 --> 01:28:08,140
Right, now like one finger lost.

211
01:28:08,140 --> 01:28:09,580
Like well, what exactly,

212
01:28:09,580 --> 01:28:11,020
and what was the other person gonna do to me

213
01:28:11,020 --> 01:28:13,940
that was so terrible that it was worth losing this finger?

214
01:28:17,020 --> 01:28:20,700
So, to me, this is like Hobbes' scenario

215
01:28:20,700 --> 01:28:23,340
was interesting because almost everyone who reads it

216
01:28:23,340 --> 01:28:26,580
acts like he is correctly describing what would occur.

217
01:28:26,580 --> 01:28:28,300
And again, he's just describing two people

218
01:28:28,300 --> 01:28:31,700
without any garment bumping into each other in the forest

219
01:28:31,700 --> 01:28:34,540
and he sure seems to claim that they will definitely go

220
01:28:34,540 --> 01:28:36,260
and fight to the death on sight.

221
01:28:37,340 --> 01:28:39,220
And that seems like a very strange prediction

222
01:28:39,220 --> 01:28:41,100
both for game theoretic terms,

223
01:28:41,100 --> 01:28:42,900
namely like you're ignoring these benefits

224
01:28:42,900 --> 01:28:45,620
and also you're ignoring the possibility

225
01:28:45,620 --> 01:28:47,660
that you've been injured during the fight.

226
01:28:47,660 --> 01:28:49,020
So, I mean like if you just said

227
01:28:49,020 --> 01:28:50,500
they will immediately run away from each other

228
01:28:50,500 --> 01:28:53,500
for fear of injury, that would make a bit more sense to me.

229
01:28:54,340 --> 01:28:55,500
Right, you know, at least,

230
01:28:55,500 --> 01:28:58,780
like you're just like, oh my God, run.

231
01:28:58,780 --> 01:29:04,780
And that actually, I think that is more common in, you know,

232
01:29:04,780 --> 01:29:08,100
so when there are unexpected meetings

233
01:29:08,100 --> 01:29:11,860
between people in different groups in isolated settings,

234
01:29:11,860 --> 01:29:14,660
very common they both run away in terror.

235
01:29:14,660 --> 01:29:16,900
But you're like, I don't know that person, he might kill me.

236
01:29:16,900 --> 01:29:18,860
But the reaction of, and therefore,

237
01:29:18,860 --> 01:29:20,740
let me go and immediately fight this person

238
01:29:20,740 --> 01:29:21,900
who might kill me.

239
01:29:21,900 --> 01:29:24,060
It's like, well, why don't you just try running away?

240
01:29:24,060 --> 01:29:25,820
Maybe that would work better.

241
01:29:25,820 --> 01:29:28,780
So, like doesn't seem to be in Hobbes either.

242
01:29:29,860 --> 01:29:34,100
All right, so anyway, when you think about a situation,

243
01:29:34,100 --> 01:29:36,340
the equilibrium that Hobbes describes

244
01:29:36,340 --> 01:29:37,500
sounds like prisoner's dilemma,

245
01:29:37,500 --> 01:29:41,820
the actual situation seems much more like the Hawk-Dove game.

246
01:29:41,820 --> 01:29:43,620
And again, then why isn't a guy as smart

247
01:29:43,620 --> 01:29:46,300
as Hobbes correctly solve the model?

248
01:29:46,300 --> 01:29:47,620
Well, there's no game theory at the time,

249
01:29:47,620 --> 01:29:49,460
so he's just working it through verbally.

250
01:29:49,460 --> 01:29:53,060
And if you know anything else about Hobbes,

251
01:29:53,060 --> 01:29:55,380
he has a very dark personality.

252
01:29:55,380 --> 01:29:58,820
So, and he did live through the English Civil War.

253
01:29:59,860 --> 01:30:01,660
So he did see a lot of horrible stuff,

254
01:30:01,660 --> 01:30:03,980
although I'd still say that if you step back

255
01:30:03,980 --> 01:30:05,140
and thought about what occurred

256
01:30:05,140 --> 01:30:07,140
during that horrible Civil War that he saw,

257
01:30:07,140 --> 01:30:08,340
did he actually see what happens

258
01:30:08,340 --> 01:30:09,820
when totally isolated individuals

259
01:30:09,820 --> 01:30:11,820
meet each other in the wilderness?

260
01:30:11,820 --> 01:30:14,220
No, he saw what happens when groups

261
01:30:14,220 --> 01:30:16,380
who developed a strong group identity

262
01:30:16,380 --> 01:30:20,020
and antipathy towards the outsider meet each other.

263
01:30:20,020 --> 01:30:22,380
So not about isolated individuals,

264
01:30:22,380 --> 01:30:24,300
but rather about what happened,

265
01:30:24,300 --> 01:30:26,460
a story about group psychology,

266
01:30:26,460 --> 01:30:27,660
which is again a very different story

267
01:30:27,660 --> 01:30:29,340
about why you get bloodshed.

268
01:30:31,580 --> 01:30:34,900
And again, so other interesting things about Hobbes is

269
01:30:36,940 --> 01:30:38,460
people who are actually involved in war

270
01:30:38,460 --> 01:30:42,580
have often noted that you have to really watch your soldiers

271
01:30:42,580 --> 01:30:44,780
because a lot of the, most people are like,

272
01:30:44,780 --> 01:30:46,820
whatever they say are very cowardly,

273
01:30:47,700 --> 01:30:49,500
especially when their life is on the line,

274
01:30:49,500 --> 01:30:51,380
they run and try to live.

275
01:30:51,380 --> 01:30:53,940
Instead of doing the right thing

276
01:30:53,940 --> 01:30:56,700
of trying to avoid dying for their cause.

277
01:30:56,700 --> 01:31:00,020
So it's very normal to go and watch soldiers very closely

278
01:31:00,020 --> 01:31:01,380
to make sure they're not running away.

279
01:31:01,380 --> 01:31:03,820
And the worse the situation gets,

280
01:31:03,820 --> 01:31:06,300
