01:41:28,680 --> 01:41:31,400
one good bet is to go and steal some of their drugs.

42
01:41:31,400 --> 01:41:33,200
You say, well, if it's not recognized,

43
01:41:33,200 --> 01:41:35,200
it's recognized by us.

44
01:41:35,200 --> 01:41:39,000
So you better watch it.

45
01:41:39,000 --> 01:41:41,600
All right, questions, questions?

46
01:41:41,600 --> 01:41:43,240
All right.

47
01:41:43,240 --> 01:41:45,240
So and then the last topic.

48
01:41:45,240 --> 01:41:47,720
So rent-seeking and lobbying inefficiency.

49
01:41:47,720 --> 01:41:50,320
Remember, we already talked about two kinds

50
01:41:50,320 --> 01:41:51,120
of inefficiencies.

51
01:41:51,120 --> 01:41:53,000
We talked about allocative inefficiency

52
01:41:53,000 --> 01:41:55,440
and productive inefficiency.

53
01:41:55,440 --> 01:41:57,440
All right.

54
01:42:02,000 --> 01:42:03,640
So the allocative inefficiency is

55
01:42:03,640 --> 01:42:06,280
the kind that gets almost all attention in textbooks,

56
01:42:06,280 --> 01:42:08,120
especially I.O. textbooks.

57
01:42:08,120 --> 01:42:11,560
So where you start off with this is your marital cost,

58
01:42:11,560 --> 01:42:14,640
this is demand, and then we've got a monopoly here.

59
01:42:14,640 --> 01:42:17,040
So we are pricing up here.

60
01:42:17,040 --> 01:42:20,960
And then we have this triangle of deadweight loss,

61
01:42:20,960 --> 01:42:24,640
which is the allocative inefficiency.

62
01:42:24,640 --> 01:42:29,680
And then we talked about the further inefficiency caused

63
01:42:29,680 --> 01:42:34,960
by having costs higher than they socially need to be.

64
01:42:34,960 --> 01:42:38,160
So for example, this could be the marginal cost

65
01:42:38,160 --> 01:42:40,920
of the brother-in-law of the dictator.

66
01:42:40,920 --> 01:42:44,640
But here is the marginal cost of the most efficient firm.

67
01:42:44,640 --> 01:42:46,160
And then as we saw, there's going

68
01:42:46,160 --> 01:42:48,800
to be another set of inefficiency

69
01:42:48,800 --> 01:42:51,760
here in the shape of a trapezoid.

70
01:42:51,760 --> 01:42:55,240
And this is the productive inefficiency.

71
01:42:55,240 --> 01:42:57,600
So this is all the inefficiency associated

72
01:42:57,600 --> 01:43:01,400
with the fact that costs are higher than they socially

73
01:43:01,400 --> 01:43:01,840
need to be.

74
01:43:05,720 --> 01:43:07,320
So we've got a trapezoid and we've

75
01:43:07,320 --> 01:43:09,600
got a triangle inefficiency.

76
01:43:09,600 --> 01:43:12,600
Now, there is another kind of inefficiency

77
01:43:12,600 --> 01:43:15,000
that Gordon Tullock is famously associated with.

78
01:43:15,000 --> 01:43:17,480
Although there's a bunch of other people that followed up

79
01:43:17,480 --> 01:43:20,040
on him as to whether or not they were actually

80
01:43:20,040 --> 01:43:22,800
copying him and republishing or whether they're

81
01:43:22,800 --> 01:43:23,760
independent discoverers.

82
01:43:23,760 --> 01:43:25,200
We may never know.

83
01:43:25,200 --> 01:43:27,640
But in any case, final form of inefficiency

84
01:43:27,640 --> 01:43:31,360
usually known as lobbying or rent-seeking inefficiency.

85
01:43:31,360 --> 01:43:34,880
So it arises when people use resources to cause

86
01:43:34,880 --> 01:43:37,320
the transfer of resources.

87
01:43:37,320 --> 01:43:40,680
So when people actually spend something

88
01:43:40,680 --> 01:43:45,920
in order to gain control of a fixed pie of stuff.

89
01:43:45,920 --> 01:43:48,760
And the simplest example would be something like this.

90
01:43:48,760 --> 01:43:52,200
So suppose that rather than the brother-in-law just

91
01:43:52,200 --> 01:43:55,560
getting the monopoly for free, instead you

92
01:43:55,560 --> 01:43:58,560
have to go and fight in the legislature.

93
01:43:58,560 --> 01:44:01,880
So you have to go and hire a bunch of lawyers

94
01:44:01,880 --> 01:44:04,320
and you have to bribe a bunch of congressmen.

95
01:44:04,320 --> 01:44:08,400
Or you have to go and hire some researchers to argue

96
01:44:08,400 --> 01:44:09,520
on your behalf.

97
01:44:09,520 --> 01:44:12,680
Or maybe you need to go and take out a big advertising campaign.

98
01:44:12,680 --> 01:44:14,480
So you have to put up resources in order

99
01:44:14,480 --> 01:44:17,160
to become the monopolist.

100
01:44:17,160 --> 01:44:22,560
Now in the simple model, this rectangle here is what?

101
01:44:22,560 --> 01:44:24,200
In the simple, so not with rent-seeking,

102
01:44:24,200 --> 01:44:25,680
but the very simple story monopoly,

103
01:44:25,680 --> 01:44:27,280
what is that rectangle?

104
01:44:27,280 --> 01:44:28,200
Profit.

105
01:44:28,200 --> 01:44:28,760
Yeah, profit.

106
01:44:28,760 --> 01:44:31,400
That rectangle is normally monopoly profits.

107
01:44:31,400 --> 01:44:35,160
But if you get this monopoly profit

108
01:44:35,160 --> 01:44:37,080
by becoming a monopolist, there is

109
01:44:37,080 --> 01:44:38,800
a reason to spend resources in order

110
01:44:38,800 --> 01:44:40,640
to get that monopoly profit.

111
01:44:40,640 --> 01:44:44,760
And so this goes from being all monopoly profit to just

112
01:44:44,760 --> 01:44:46,720
being the gross monopoly profit.

113
01:44:46,720 --> 01:44:49,920
It's a monopoly profit that you get if you win.

114
01:44:49,920 --> 01:44:51,480
But in order to win, you might have

115
01:44:51,480 --> 01:44:55,240
to spend a lot of resources.

116
01:44:55,240 --> 01:44:57,000
And those resources get burned up.

117
01:44:57,000 --> 01:45:00,080
They're not actually going to be enjoyed by anyone in society.

118
01:45:02,600 --> 01:45:06,680
So just imagine a world where the more you spend

119
01:45:06,680 --> 01:45:09,080
on your lobbying efforts to convince the world

120
01:45:09,080 --> 01:45:11,080
that you deserve to be a monopolist,

121
01:45:11,080 --> 01:45:14,200
the more you prove your chances.

122
01:45:14,200 --> 01:45:17,400
And we can picture this like a tug-of-war contest.

123
01:45:17,400 --> 01:45:20,040
So the game tug-of-war where there's a rope,

124
01:45:20,040 --> 01:45:22,120
and then there's a thing of mud in the center.

125
01:45:22,120 --> 01:45:26,000
And then you have people more pulling the rope.

126
01:45:28,880 --> 01:45:31,400
And then imagine that each side can go and hire

127
01:45:31,400 --> 01:45:34,240
more assistant rope pullers.

128
01:45:34,240 --> 01:45:36,280
And you can end, you know, that's really bad.

129
01:45:36,280 --> 01:45:37,280
Like, it's not just mud.

130
01:45:37,280 --> 01:45:39,960
Maybe it's like molten lava.

131
01:45:39,960 --> 01:45:42,280
All right, so you can keep adding more and more people.

132
01:45:42,280 --> 01:45:44,800
Each side will keep adding more and more people.

133
01:45:44,800 --> 01:45:52,600
And when in equilibrium does the expected benefits

134
01:45:52,600 --> 01:45:55,840
of participating in this process go down to just the normal

135
01:45:55,840 --> 01:45:58,160
level?

136
01:45:58,160 --> 01:46:00,120
So anyway, Gordon Tollick's deep insight here

137
01:46:00,120 --> 01:46:02,280
is we can think about this as just

138
01:46:02,280 --> 01:46:04,480
being a competitive industry.

139
01:46:04,480 --> 01:46:06,400
In competitive industry, in the long run,

140
01:46:06,400 --> 01:46:08,160
price is equal to what again?

141
01:46:08,160 --> 01:46:09,320
Yeah, margin loss.

142
01:46:09,320 --> 01:46:13,320
But also, more importantly in this case, average cost.

143
01:46:13,320 --> 01:46:15,840
So you keep getting entry in the long run

144
01:46:15,840 --> 01:46:19,360
until price is going to be equal to average cost.

145
01:46:19,360 --> 01:46:23,600
So what this means is that if initially the rate of return

146
01:46:23,600 --> 01:46:26,280
to fighting over those monopoly profits is 10%,

147
01:46:26,280 --> 01:46:27,840
this will give you more entry.

148
01:46:27,840 --> 01:46:29,880
And then finally, you will get entry

149
01:46:29,880 --> 01:46:34,080
until the rate of return in the lobbying industry.

150
01:46:34,080 --> 01:46:36,040
We'll call it industry metaphorically

151
01:46:36,040 --> 01:46:38,480
because it's not very industrious.

152
01:46:38,480 --> 01:46:40,480
It doesn't lead to production, but it

153
01:46:40,480 --> 01:46:42,760
does lead to redistribution.

154
01:46:42,760 --> 01:46:47,760
You get more and more entry until our economic profits are

155
01:46:47,760 --> 01:46:48,640
what?

156
01:46:48,640 --> 01:46:49,160
Zero.

157
01:46:49,160 --> 01:46:52,480
Yes, economic profits are zero.

158
01:46:52,480 --> 01:46:53,320
OK.

159
01:46:53,320 --> 01:46:56,400
And so that means that rather than this thing here

160
01:46:56,400 --> 01:47:00,680
being true monopoly profits, that actually we

161
01:47:00,680 --> 01:47:03,040
can go and think about this as yet another kind

162
01:47:03,040 --> 01:47:05,960
of deadweight loss.

163
01:47:05,960 --> 01:47:08,840
That whole thing can become deadweight loss.

164
01:47:08,840 --> 01:47:10,600
Right?

165
01:47:10,600 --> 01:47:14,320
So if there's a million dollars worth of benefit

166
01:47:14,320 --> 01:47:17,120
from getting the monopoly grant, then in equilibrium

167
01:47:17,120 --> 01:47:20,080
it seems like the total amount that people spend on lobbying

168
01:47:20,080 --> 01:47:22,320
will be a million dollars.

169
01:47:22,320 --> 01:47:23,920
Now, of course, the firm that wins

170
01:47:23,920 --> 01:47:25,680
is going to do way better than that.

171
01:47:25,680 --> 01:47:28,160
But on the other hand, the firms that lose

172
01:47:28,160 --> 01:47:29,680
are going to be losing money.

173
01:47:29,680 --> 01:47:32,560
I mean, it's just like, say, a patent race, where

174
01:47:32,560 --> 01:47:35,080
whoever gets there first and wins,

175
01:47:35,080 --> 01:47:37,440
they're going to be making good profits.

176
01:47:37,440 --> 01:47:39,320
But when you think about the social cost,

177
01:47:39,320 --> 01:47:41,600
you also need to subtract out all of the research

178
01:47:41,600 --> 01:47:44,960
efforts of all the losers who get no gross profits

179
01:47:44,960 --> 01:47:47,520
and still had all the research costs.

180
01:47:47,520 --> 01:47:48,000
Right?

181
01:47:48,000 --> 01:47:51,200
And in equilibrium, we need to have price equals average cost.

182
01:47:51,200 --> 01:47:53,920
So the total amount of resources that people

183
01:47:53,920 --> 01:47:56,400
spend trying to get the monopoly profit

184
01:47:56,400 --> 01:48:00,320
will actually equal the monopoly profits.

185
01:48:00,320 --> 01:48:02,480
So anyway, this is a lot like mixed value reasoning.

186
01:48:02,480 --> 01:48:04,640
So in equilibrium, the payoffs of production and redistribution

187
01:48:04,640 --> 01:48:05,640
are going to be equal.

188
01:48:05,640 --> 01:48:07,120
And you keep entering this arms race

189
01:48:07,120 --> 01:48:08,880
until the net profits of the privilege

190
01:48:08,880 --> 01:48:12,640
are zero, which happens when the total cost of lobbying

191
01:48:12,640 --> 01:48:15,400
equals the total value of the monopoly privilege.

192
01:48:15,400 --> 01:48:17,200
And this has a special name.

193
01:48:17,200 --> 01:48:19,720
So again, if this doesn't need to happen,

194
01:48:19,720 --> 01:48:23,320
so if that does happen, then we call it full rent dissipation.

195
01:48:29,240 --> 01:48:31,080
And this here has a special name.

196
01:48:31,080 --> 01:48:32,240
It's not just a rectangle.

197
01:48:32,240 --> 01:48:36,880
It's the telec rectangle.

198
01:48:36,880 --> 01:48:38,280
Geometrically, the telec rectangle

199
01:48:38,280 --> 01:48:40,760
is exactly the same as the monopoly profit rectangle,

200
01:48:40,760 --> 01:48:45,440
only we now can reinterpret it as being deadweight loss

201
01:48:45,440 --> 01:48:48,640
rather than transfer.

202
01:48:48,640 --> 01:48:49,120
Right?

203
01:48:49,120 --> 01:48:51,120
And now, look at how gloomy this market is.

204
01:48:51,120 --> 01:48:53,840
We started off with a market where the total social surplus

205
01:48:53,840 --> 01:48:56,080
was this giant triangle.

206
01:48:56,080 --> 01:48:58,640
And we end up with this little stub.

207
01:48:58,640 --> 01:49:03,080
We end up with that little stub of surplus at the end.

208
01:49:03,080 --> 01:49:07,160
So this is what's left over after an incompetent firm

209
01:49:07,160 --> 01:49:09,320
jacks up the price after fighting

210
01:49:09,320 --> 01:49:10,320
for the right to do so.

211
01:49:12,960 --> 01:49:14,640
And then, yeah, there's still something.

212
01:49:14,640 --> 01:49:16,520
So there's still better to have a product that

213
01:49:16,520 --> 01:49:17,760
exists than not exist.

214
01:49:17,760 --> 01:49:22,160
But the social gain that we just realized

215
01:49:22,160 --> 01:49:26,160
when we compare it to the potential is very small.

216
01:49:26,160 --> 01:49:31,040
So there, I guess it looks like 1 9th, something like that.

217
01:49:31,040 --> 01:49:32,640
As drawn, these are similar triangles.

218
01:49:32,640 --> 01:49:34,600
You can go and do a little bit like that.

219
01:49:38,600 --> 01:49:39,440
All right.

220
01:49:39,440 --> 01:49:40,720
Questions?

221
01:49:40,720 --> 01:49:41,220
Yes?

222
01:49:41,220 --> 01:49:45,120
So that whole bottom trapezoid, that's all productive?

223
01:49:45,120 --> 01:49:47,160
Yes, that's all productive inefficiency, exactly.

224
01:49:47,160 --> 01:49:52,040
So is this more of a competitive market?

225
01:49:52,040 --> 01:49:54,320
Yes, so the idea is that in a competitive market,

226
01:49:54,320 --> 01:49:57,200
you could have this thing could have happened.

227
01:49:57,200 --> 01:49:57,760
Yes.

228
01:49:57,760 --> 01:50:00,680
So again, I suppose if there were two firms that

229
01:50:00,680 --> 01:50:03,920
both had that, or if one firm had these costs,

230
01:50:03,920 --> 01:50:06,320
but there was a potential one that was just as efficient,

231
01:50:06,320 --> 01:50:08,560
then you actually could very well be right there.

232
01:50:12,480 --> 01:50:15,160
Now, when people think about this situation,

233
01:50:15,160 --> 01:50:17,240
common reaction is, well, we wouldn't

234
01:50:17,240 --> 01:50:19,720
have to go and deal with this problem.

235
01:50:19,720 --> 01:50:22,560
It wouldn't be a deadweight loss of the tele-rectangle

236
01:50:22,560 --> 01:50:26,800
if instead of having lobbying, we just did competitive bidding.

237
01:50:26,800 --> 01:50:32,080
So suppose that instead of having a giant argument or fight

238
01:50:32,080 --> 01:50:34,920
about who gets the monopoly profits,

239
01:50:34,920 --> 01:50:38,080
instead we just had the government auction it.

240
01:50:38,080 --> 01:50:40,120
Then the government would raise this revenue,

241
01:50:40,120 --> 01:50:42,040
and that would be a transfer to the government

242
01:50:42,040 --> 01:50:45,720
rather than a deadweight loss to society.

243
01:50:45,720 --> 01:50:48,360
OK, now, what Tulloch pointed out,

244
01:50:48,360 --> 01:50:53,240
though, is that there really is a law of conservation

245
01:50:53,240 --> 01:50:56,760
of rent-seeking, because if the government is able to go and get

246
01:50:56,760 --> 01:50:58,880
all these monopoly profits, this is just

247
01:50:58,880 --> 01:51:00,640
going to increase competition to become

248
01:51:00,640 --> 01:51:02,320
the people in charge of the government who

249
01:51:02,320 --> 01:51:04,360
are able to get the money.

250
01:51:04,360 --> 01:51:06,520
So you can very easily see this in things

251
01:51:06,520 --> 01:51:11,760
like medieval monarchies, where on the one hand,

252
01:51:11,760 --> 01:51:14,280
if you are the king, you get all this great stuff.

253
01:51:14,280 --> 01:51:17,840
But that means that other people look at you with envy

254
01:51:17,840 --> 01:51:20,720
and say, if only I could become the king.

255
01:51:20,720 --> 01:51:22,320
I could be the king.

256
01:51:22,320 --> 01:51:23,600
I should be the king.

257
01:51:23,600 --> 01:51:25,840
If I would just raise an army and kill

258
01:51:25,840 --> 01:51:27,800
the people in charge, that would be me.

259
01:51:27,800 --> 01:51:29,880
Of course, you're not the only one thinking that.

260
01:51:29,880 --> 01:51:31,680
There's a lot of people thinking that.

261
01:51:31,680 --> 01:51:35,000
So if you know the show or books Game of Thrones,

262
01:51:35,000 --> 01:51:36,720
there's enormous surplus.

263
01:51:36,720 --> 01:51:38,320
But basically, it looks like it's

264
01:51:38,320 --> 01:51:40,200
all going to be dissipated.

265
01:51:40,200 --> 01:51:42,480
There's going to be the whole area will be totally laid

266
01:51:42,480 --> 01:51:43,600
waste.

267
01:51:43,600 --> 01:51:45,200
I don't know the final outcome.

268
01:51:45,200 --> 01:51:50,280
But realistically, it just seems like that's it for them.

269
01:51:50,280 --> 01:51:52,160
But I suppose that wouldn't really

270
01:51:52,160 --> 01:51:54,280
be a good ending for the story.

271
01:51:54,280 --> 01:51:57,640
But the idea is that if it's really good to be the king,

272
01:51:57,640 --> 01:52:00,720
then people will fight very bitterly to become the king.

273
01:52:00,720 --> 01:52:04,200
So the king can very peaceably go and raise lots of revenue

274
01:52:04,200 --> 01:52:06,080
from auctioning off monopolies.

275
01:52:06,080 --> 01:52:08,280
There will be other people around, interesting

276
01:52:08,280 --> 01:52:10,560
and very brutally, taking over that job.

277
01:52:10,560 --> 01:52:13,640
So Game of Thrones is based on the War of the Roses.

278
01:52:13,640 --> 01:52:16,240
So if you ever looked at the fraction

279
01:52:16,240 --> 01:52:18,720
of the warring families that survived at the end of the war,

280
01:52:18,720 --> 01:52:21,320
I think like 90% of them were dead by the end.

281
01:52:21,320 --> 01:52:23,840
So they were killing each other left and right.

282
01:52:23,840 --> 01:52:26,040
So if only they could have just lived in peace,

283
01:52:26,040 --> 01:52:27,520
things would have been a lot better.

284
01:52:27,520 --> 01:52:30,560
But instead, red dissipation came along.

285
01:52:30,560 --> 01:52:33,240
And they fought for it.

286
01:52:33,240 --> 01:52:35,360
Now, another example of red dissipation

287
01:52:35,360 --> 01:52:37,360
that Talhah would like to talk about.

288
01:52:37,360 --> 01:52:39,120
So Talhah's family, the family of Talhah,

289
01:52:39,120 --> 01:52:40,560
he would like to talk about.

290
01:52:40,560 --> 01:52:42,960
So Talhah spent quite a few years in China

291
01:52:42,960 --> 01:52:45,160
during World War II and after World War II.

292
01:52:45,160 --> 01:52:49,880
So he'd like to talk about the system of the Chinese civil

293
01:52:49,880 --> 01:52:51,320
service exams.

294
01:52:51,320 --> 01:52:53,320
So people often talk about how China

295
01:52:53,320 --> 01:52:55,920
had these impartial civil service exams.

296
01:52:55,920 --> 01:52:57,520
But the content of the exams was not

297
01:52:57,520 --> 01:52:59,280
on anything in social science or anything

298
01:52:59,280 --> 01:53:01,600
like that that would seem to improve your ability to do

299
01:53:01,600 --> 01:53:02,100
the job.

300
01:53:02,100 --> 01:53:04,480
Instead, it was basically just exams

301
01:53:04,480 --> 01:53:08,360
about what long-dead classical thinkers said,

302
01:53:08,360 --> 01:53:10,920
what Confucius said, things like this.

303
01:53:10,920 --> 01:53:15,520
I once talked to a guy who had gone to, let's see.

304
01:53:15,520 --> 01:53:17,920
So he'd actually done 20 years in a slave labor

305
01:53:17,920 --> 01:53:19,360
camps under Mao.

306
01:53:19,360 --> 01:53:21,560
But before that, he'd been at the International School

307
01:53:21,560 --> 01:53:22,520
in Beijing.

308
01:53:22,520 --> 01:53:24,320
But he said that his parents also

309
01:53:24,320 --> 01:53:26,800
made him go and do Confucius and get

310
01:53:26,800 --> 01:53:29,280
to be prepared for the civil service exams

311
01:53:29,280 --> 01:53:30,680
if they ever came back.

312
01:53:30,680 --> 01:53:32,680
But he was saying, so I heard that they was just

313
01:53:32,680 --> 01:53:33,720
memorizing Confucius.

314
01:53:33,720 --> 01:53:34,360
Is that true?

315
01:53:34,360 --> 01:53:35,400
Yeah, that's what it was.

316
01:53:35,400 --> 01:53:37,200
He was memorizing Confucius.

317
01:53:37,200 --> 01:53:39,240
But anyway, so what Tullock said was

318
01:53:39,240 --> 01:53:42,360
that under the old imperial system,

319
01:53:42,360 --> 01:53:44,840
villages would look around for a really smart kid.

320
01:53:44,840 --> 01:53:46,360
If they could find one, the village

321
01:53:46,360 --> 01:53:49,640
would pour all these resources into hiring tutors and so on

322
01:53:49,640 --> 01:53:52,060
so the kid could get a really high score on the civil service

323
01:53:52,060 --> 01:53:54,160
test and then get a great job.

324
01:53:54,160 --> 01:53:55,600
And then what is the kid supposed

325
01:53:55,600 --> 01:53:57,160
to do once he gets this great job?

326
01:53:57,160 --> 01:53:58,640
Bring us back delicious rents.

327
01:53:58,640 --> 01:54:01,240
Yes, funnel money back to the home village.

328
01:54:01,240 --> 01:54:03,720
So give all the money back to the home village.

329
01:54:03,720 --> 01:54:05,920
And so you do have a competitive system.

330
01:54:05,920 --> 01:54:08,480
But it's one where you burn an enormous amount of resources

331
01:54:08,480 --> 01:54:10,720
as well as all the smartest people in the country,

332
01:54:10,720 --> 01:54:13,800
studying subjects of very little practical relevance,

333
01:54:13,800 --> 01:54:14,760
from what I've heard.

334
01:54:14,760 --> 01:54:15,680
I did read Confucius.

335
01:54:15,680 --> 01:54:17,160
I just feel, at least the analects,

336
01:54:17,160 --> 01:54:19,800
this does not seem very useful for anything.

337
01:54:19,800 --> 01:54:22,440
The right number of steps to walk through.

338
01:54:22,440 --> 01:54:23,720
I don't know.

339
01:54:23,720 --> 01:54:25,200
Maybe there's something I'm missing.

340
01:54:25,200 --> 01:54:28,840
But it sure didn't seem like it was some kind of proto-economics

341
01:54:28,840 --> 01:54:33,160
or anything else, cost-benefit analysis, or even game theory.

342
01:54:33,160 --> 01:54:34,560
No, none of those things.

343
01:54:34,560 --> 01:54:37,520
So anyway, so this is a story, again,

344
01:54:37,520 --> 01:54:39,320
of rent dissipation, where people

345
01:54:39,320 --> 01:54:43,040
know that if you become a civil servant by passing this test,

346
01:54:43,040 --> 01:54:45,040
then you are able to bring home lots of rewards

347
01:54:45,040 --> 01:54:45,880
to your village.

348
01:54:45,880 --> 01:54:48,600
So they pour all these brains and all these resources

349
01:54:48,600 --> 01:54:50,600
into getting that job.

350
01:54:50,600 --> 01:54:55,920
So whatever system we use for handing out the rents,

351
01:54:55,920 --> 01:54:58,480
people then pour resources into gaining that system.

352
01:55:02,440 --> 01:55:04,080
So people members in politics, they'll

353
01:55:04,080 --> 01:55:05,920
pay more to win a seat.

354
01:55:05,920 --> 01:55:08,720
And of course, this works in a dictatorship or monarchy.

355
01:55:08,720 --> 01:55:11,200
So if you get rich by handing out monopoly privileges,

356
01:55:11,200 --> 01:55:13,640
then this strengthens the incentives upstarts to seize

357
01:55:13,640 --> 01:55:17,120
a coup, to seize this realm, seize your coup, and so on.

358
01:55:17,120 --> 01:55:21,640
So if you could actually go and get a really stable monarchy,

359
01:55:21,640 --> 01:55:24,600
where no one thinks the rebellion would ever happen,

360
01:55:24,600 --> 01:55:26,000
then maybe.

361
01:55:26,000 --> 01:55:28,760
Although even there, there are always workarounds.

362
01:55:28,760 --> 01:55:31,960
So I think the Japanese emperor, the line

363
01:55:31,960 --> 01:55:35,040
was unbroken for thousands of years.

364
01:55:35,040 --> 01:55:37,720
But there were still all sorts of civil wars.

365
01:55:37,720 --> 01:55:40,240
How can you have civil wars, but the same guy, always

366
01:55:40,240 --> 01:55:42,640
killing, the same line, always stays in power?

367
01:55:42,640 --> 01:55:44,160
And the answer is, there are civil wars

368
01:55:44,160 --> 01:55:46,800
over who gets to marry the emperor.

369
01:55:46,800 --> 01:55:49,720
And the winner says, all right, emperor, lord of all the world,

370
01:55:49,720 --> 01:55:53,120
you're marrying our daughter now, or else.

371
01:55:53,120 --> 01:55:54,840
And also, all your relatives are going

372
01:55:54,840 --> 01:55:57,200
to marry all of our people here.

373
01:55:57,200 --> 01:55:58,960
And in this way, you can gradually

374
01:55:58,960 --> 01:56:02,480
do a genetic takeover of the imperial house,

375
01:56:02,480 --> 01:56:05,600
even though there is an unbroken line of succession.

376
01:56:05,600 --> 01:56:08,000
You can have massive bloodshed, even when

377
01:56:08,000 --> 01:56:10,280
there's no totally clear at all times

378
01:56:10,280 --> 01:56:13,640
what the chain of what the line of succession is.

379
01:56:13,640 --> 01:56:14,760
All right.

380
01:56:14,760 --> 01:56:17,240
So and then a repeat play, could we do a better equilibrium?

381
01:56:17,240 --> 01:56:19,480
But as usual, not necessarily.

382
01:56:19,480 --> 01:56:22,440
Firm might lobby extra hard to get reputation for toughness.

383
01:56:22,440 --> 01:56:25,680
You might lobby way past the point of profitability

384
01:56:25,680 --> 01:56:27,800
just to scare people.

385
01:56:27,800 --> 01:56:29,560
They never try to lobby against us.

386
01:56:29,560 --> 01:56:33,200
We'll just pour as much money in as much as it takes.

387
01:56:33,200 --> 01:56:33,760
All right.

388
01:56:33,760 --> 01:56:36,360
So questions about this?

389
01:56:36,360 --> 01:56:42,800
So again, this is the ultimate pessimistic diagram of markets,

390
01:56:42,800 --> 01:56:45,400
because it shows where all three kinds of inefficiency

391
01:56:45,400 --> 01:56:48,760
are piled on top of each other.

392
01:56:48,760 --> 01:56:51,080
But on the other hand, you say it's also how much better

393
01:56:51,080 --> 01:56:52,480
the world can be.

394
01:56:52,480 --> 01:56:53,520
So yes.

395
01:56:53,520 --> 01:56:56,160
So any time you just say the world right now

396
01:56:56,160 --> 01:56:59,800
is way below potential, in a way that's very optimistic,

397
01:56:59,800 --> 01:57:01,480
because there's so many gains.

398
01:57:01,480 --> 01:57:05,440
It could be fantastic if only people would learn economics

399
01:57:05,440 --> 01:57:07,400
and act upon it.

400
01:57:07,400 --> 01:57:09,200
That's nice.

401
01:57:09,200 --> 01:57:09,960
All right.

402
01:57:09,960 --> 01:57:14,120
So questions about rent seeking and all?

403
01:57:14,120 --> 01:57:15,920
Now, then let's talk about the exam.

404
01:57:15,920 --> 01:57:17,680
And then I'll take any questions that you

405
01:57:17,680 --> 01:57:20,800
might have about any material.

406
01:57:20,800 --> 01:57:24,040
So the basics of the exam.

407
01:57:24,040 --> 01:57:26,520
OK.

408
01:57:26,520 --> 01:57:27,920
All right.

409
01:57:27,920 --> 01:57:29,680
So the exam will be next class.

410
01:57:29,680 --> 01:57:31,560
You know that, right?

411
01:57:31,560 --> 01:57:36,040
And the best way of studying is to take

412
01:57:36,040 --> 01:57:39,000
old exams under time pressure.

413
01:57:39,000 --> 01:57:40,800
Take old exams under time pressure.

414
01:57:40,800 --> 01:57:44,280
There are 14 old exams up there.

415
01:57:44,280 --> 01:57:47,680
So the early ones are just a bit longer than the one

416
01:57:47,680 --> 01:57:48,720
