01:59:11,120 --> 01:59:14,280
So yes, you are allowed to print off your entire hard drive

448
01:59:14,280 --> 01:59:15,560
if you want.

449
01:59:15,560 --> 01:59:17,600
So that's perfectly fine.

450
01:59:17,600 --> 01:59:20,720
If you look at the amount of time you'll have,

451
01:59:20,720 --> 01:59:22,160
it's not super rushed.

452
01:59:22,160 --> 01:59:26,120
But if you want to just take your chances

453
01:59:26,120 --> 01:59:28,160
and not do the readings and then rush through them

454
01:59:28,160 --> 01:59:29,800
during the exam, that's possible.

455
01:59:29,800 --> 01:59:33,240
Not only I wouldn't recommend it.

456
01:59:33,240 --> 01:59:35,360
So mostly, this is just if you're

457
01:59:35,360 --> 01:59:37,840
someone who draws a blank under pressure,

458
01:59:37,840 --> 01:59:39,520
oh my god, I forgot, what is this?

459
01:59:39,520 --> 01:59:41,120
And you can go and check your notes,

460
01:59:41,120 --> 01:59:45,680
and then you can show what you actually know.

461
01:59:45,680 --> 01:59:49,800
I'm trying to reduce the effect of a nervous personality

462
01:59:49,800 --> 01:59:54,200
during an exam or just sudden stress prone amnesia.

463
01:59:54,200 --> 01:59:58,280
Now, since I am letting you have all your books and notes,

464
01:59:58,280 --> 01:60:00,160
and of course, since this is a PhD class,


1
01:60:00,000 --> 01:60:04,000
there are very few questions involving rote memorization.

2
01:60:04,000 --> 01:60:08,000
So almost all the questions require you to genuinely understand the material

3
01:60:08,000 --> 01:60:12,000
and think about it, talk about it.

4
01:60:12,000 --> 01:60:16,000
What does it mean to genuinely understand material as opposed to not really

5
01:60:16,000 --> 01:60:20,000
to only pretend to understand material. But one good test is

6
01:60:20,000 --> 01:60:24,000
if I can give you a question that is not exactly the same as one that I've already

7
01:60:24,000 --> 01:60:28,000
asked you and you can still do it. Usually it's only a small change.

8
01:60:28,000 --> 01:60:32,000
I can change the question a little bit and you can still do the question.

9
01:60:32,000 --> 01:60:36,000
That shows you really understand. If you only do exactly the questions we've done, then you don't

10
01:60:36,000 --> 01:60:40,000
really understand. Now, an important warning.

11
01:60:40,000 --> 01:60:44,000
Sometimes there will be questions that are about models.

12
01:60:44,000 --> 01:60:48,000
And when a question is about a model, you should just talk about the model.

13
01:60:48,000 --> 01:60:52,000
So in the coordination game, how many of the delivery are there? Then don't start talking about Esperanto.

14
01:60:52,000 --> 01:60:56,000
Then there's no need to

15
01:60:56,000 --> 01:61:00,000
discuss the real world. In the coordination game, there can be two pure

16
01:61:00,000 --> 01:61:04,000
strategy Nash equilibria and one mixed strategy Nash equilibria.

17
01:61:04,000 --> 01:61:08,000
But sometimes there will be questions that ask you about the real world.

18
01:61:08,000 --> 01:61:12,000
And if the question asks you about the real world, is it okay to say assume Bertrand

19
01:61:12,000 --> 01:61:16,000
or Assume Chrono Collusion

20
01:61:16,000 --> 01:61:20,000
or assume anything. If it's a question about the real world,

21
01:61:20,000 --> 01:61:24,000
you need to actually argue. So it's good to use a model from class, but you want

22
01:61:24,000 --> 01:61:28,000
to begin by arguing the model's relevance.

23
01:61:28,000 --> 01:61:32,000
Or at least close. Say, this model may not be perfect, but it's a reasonable

24
01:61:32,000 --> 01:61:36,000
approximation in this particular case we're talking about.

25
01:61:36,000 --> 01:61:40,000
Similarly, if there's a question like, is it a good model?

26
01:61:40,000 --> 01:61:44,000
In explaining the real world. You don't want to just give some circular thing

27
01:61:44,000 --> 01:61:48,000
saying, of course it's a good model because it's internally consistent.

28
01:61:48,000 --> 01:61:52,000
Instead, if the question asks you to assess the empirical relevance of the model,

29
01:61:52,000 --> 01:61:56,000
you need to understand the model well enough to see discrepancies and then

30
01:61:56,000 --> 01:62:00,000
talk about them.

31
01:62:00,000 --> 01:62:04,000
In the Berkeley competitive model,

32
01:62:04,000 --> 01:62:08,000
what is an obvious discrepancy between going to the movie theater on a bad day

33
01:62:08,000 --> 01:62:12,000
and the Berkeley competitive model?

34
01:62:12,000 --> 01:62:16,000
Lowering the price a penny.

35
01:62:16,000 --> 01:62:20,000
We see a bunch of vacant seats and the price seems to be way above

36
01:62:20,000 --> 01:62:24,000
the marginal cost.

37
01:62:24,000 --> 01:62:28,000
It's an essay question and someone wants to say, I worked in a movie theater, let me explain

38
01:62:28,000 --> 01:62:32,000
the marginal cost of letting one more person in is way higher than you think.

39
01:62:32,000 --> 01:62:36,000
Do you know what the cleanup costs are? Do you know how much one more body raises the air conditioning

40
01:62:36,000 --> 01:62:40,000
costs? Do you know what the average amount of damage that one person does

41
01:62:40,000 --> 01:62:44,000
to the screen? If you start adding it up, even if I'm a bit

42
01:62:44,000 --> 01:62:48,000
suspicious about the numbers, still, if that's your argument,

43
01:62:48,000 --> 01:62:52,000
I'm going to look at that and say, well, that seems like the person is at least on point, relevant,

44
01:62:52,000 --> 01:62:56,000
and maybe. So it seems like a pretty good essay

45
01:62:56,000 --> 01:63:00,000
anyway, so that could be a good way of doing it. So yes, the Berkeley competitive model is totally relevant

46
01:63:00,000 --> 01:63:04,000
and the price is not above marginal cost. You really understood the industry

47
01:63:04,000 --> 01:63:08,000
as I do. So you could go that route and that could be a great essay.

48
01:63:08,000 --> 01:63:12,000
But if you just go and repeat back

49
01:63:12,000 --> 01:63:16,000
what the model says and then ignore facts that would appear

50
01:63:16,000 --> 01:63:20,000
to be inconsistent with the model, on a question about the real world

51
01:63:20,000 --> 01:63:24,000
you'll do poorly. So often I will actually use the phrase

52
01:63:24,000 --> 01:63:28,000
in the real world.

53
01:63:28,000 --> 01:63:32,000
So that is a dead giveaway that I'm actually, you'll want you to go

54
01:63:32,000 --> 01:63:36,000
and compare what we've done in class to the real world.

55
01:63:36,000 --> 01:63:40,000
Although sometimes I'll also just, I will

56
01:63:40,000 --> 01:63:44,000
describe events at which are clearly supposed to be in the real world.

57
01:63:44,000 --> 01:63:48,000
I think you have a better question, like, if you were playing a repeated prisoner's lemma against another

58
01:63:48,000 --> 01:63:52,000
student in this class, how would you play and why?

59
01:63:52,000 --> 01:63:56,000
And if your answer is, I would have defected turn one

60
01:63:56,000 --> 01:64:00,000
because that's what backwards deduction says, you're not going to do very well on it.

61
01:64:00,000 --> 01:64:04,000
Because I don't think you, first because I just don't believe you.

62
01:64:04,000 --> 01:64:08,000
I don't believe you would play that way.

63
01:64:08,000 --> 01:64:12,000
Firstly, no human being does play that way. Unless you were to go

64
01:64:12,000 --> 01:64:16,000
and say, well this is the way I always do things.

65
01:64:16,000 --> 01:64:20,000
If you flesh it out and start giving details. Or if you were to even say,

66
01:64:20,000 --> 01:64:24,000
well I think that the other people in the class would not play like normal human beings and I would

67
01:64:24,000 --> 01:64:28,000
just be so worried about this that I would just not trust them.

68
01:64:28,000 --> 01:64:32,000
At least give me a story where it sounds like you

69
01:64:32,000 --> 01:64:36,000
have thought through the problem and you've understood the complexities and you're still going

70
01:64:36,000 --> 01:64:40,000
to say something strange. That's a much better thing than just saying something

71
01:64:40,000 --> 01:64:44,000
that seems like you're repeating back what a model that has

72
01:64:44,000 --> 01:64:48,000
obvious, seemingly obvious problems.

73
01:64:48,000 --> 01:64:52,000
Let's see. So we've got the breakdown,

74
01:64:52,000 --> 01:64:56,000
we've got the notes, the distinction between the real world and

75
01:64:56,000 --> 01:65:00,000
a model. So in my public choice class on the first homework

76
01:65:00,000 --> 01:65:04,000
almost everyone answered all questions as if I was asking about models even though many questions

77
01:65:04,000 --> 01:65:08,000
were clearly about the real world. Which fits my pet theory that

78
01:65:08,000 --> 01:65:12,000
the main intellectual harm economics does to people is to make

79
01:65:12,000 --> 01:65:16,000
them forget the real world. When the whole point of the subject

80
01:65:16,000 --> 01:65:20,000
is ultimately to take what we have learned and apply it to the real

81
01:65:20,000 --> 01:65:24,000
world. To understand the real world. It is kind of

82
01:65:24,000 --> 01:65:28,000
our fault for not mentioning it more.

83
01:65:28,000 --> 01:65:32,000
It seems like you should take it for granted that that is the ultimate goal

84
01:65:32,000 --> 01:65:36,000
even if we don't discuss it in every class. Let's see.

85
01:65:36,000 --> 01:65:40,000
So other things to know.

86
01:65:40,000 --> 01:65:44,000
Many people consider my exams difficult but this is

87
01:65:44,000 --> 01:65:48,000
only because they're focusing on nominal grades instead of real grades.

88
01:65:48,000 --> 01:65:52,000
I do write exams trying to get a 50% average. I try to get the maximum

89
01:65:52,000 --> 01:65:56,000
spread so I can really see the difference and people will understand the material well.

90
01:65:56,000 --> 01:66:00,000
People don't understand so well. But a 50% will generally get you about a B+.

91
